{"severity":"DEBUG","time":"2025-11-22T20:17:07+05:45","caller":"lesson_plan/node_reconciler.go:703","message":"[prompt-input-and-output]","traceID":"00000000000000000000000000000000","traceSampled":false,"requestID":"MacBook-Pro.local/fBn6sl4UuK-000001","userID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","authorizedUserID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","Context":"","Blueprint":"","maxTokens":"0","Substrand":"Probability & Statistics Foundations","YearOrder":"8","ContentDescriptor":"","ClassYear":"Grade 8","FirstSubstrand":"Basic Data Representation","user":"\n\n# LESSON INPUTS\nCountry = Australia\nClassYear = Grade 8\nContext = \nSkill = Analysing and creating frequency tables with categorical data\n\n# XML FORMAT\n- Use inline KaTeX for ANY numbers and expressions in All question and answer fields .eg year $2\\,020$, $15.6\\%$, $4$ cm$^2$, $x \\cdot b$.\n- Output strictly valid XML enclosed in <root></root> tags.\n- <EXTREMELY IMPORTANT> It is absolutely imperative that you MUST use KaTeX, Otherwise the Renderer will FAIL, ALWAYS use KaTeX in All questions and answer. </EXTREMELY IMPORTANT>\n\n<root>\n<prerequisitesChosen>first prerequisite skill</prerequisitesChosen>\n<prerequisitesChosen>second prerequisite skill</prerequisitesChosen>\n<prerequisitesChosen>third prerequisite skill</prerequisitesChosen>\n<situation>real world scenario description [ max $100$ characters - be concise and punchy ]</situation>\n<easy>\n<picked_skill>prerequisite skill from prerequisitesChosen</picked_skill>\n<questionReasoning>why this is foundational and low-floor; confirm formatting and scope</questionReasoning>\n<question>easy question [ max $120$ characters - AIM FOR 80 ]</question>\n<answerReasoning>step by step solution [ max $150$ characters ]</answerReasoning>\n<answer>exact answer in KaTeX</answer>\n</easy>\n<medium>\n<picked_skill>prerequisite skill from prerequisitesChosen</picked_skill>\n<questionReasoning>explain harder than easy via one extra step/connection; confirm scope/formatting</questionReasoning>\n<question>medium question [ max $120$ characters - AIM FOR 80 ]</question>\n<answerReasoning>step by step solution [ max $150$ characters ]</answerReasoning>\n<answer>exact answer in KaTeX</answer>\n</medium>\n<hard>\n<picked_skill>prerequisite skill from prerequisitesChosen</picked_skill>\n<questionReasoning>explain harder than medium via multi-step/combination; confirm scope/formatting</questionReasoning>\n<question>hard question [ max $120$ characters - AIM FOR 80 ]</question>\n<answerReasoning>step by step solution [ max $150$ characters ]</answerReasoning>\n<answer>exact answer in KaTeX</answer>\n</hard>\n</root>\n\n","architecture":"MoE","inferenceSpeed":"3000-tokens-per-sec","nodeType":"*lessonplan.WarmUpQuestionsWithContextNode","Skills":"Analysing and creating frequency tables with categorical data","rawOutput":"<root>\n<prerequisitesChosen>Basic addition of whole numbers</prerequisitesChosen>\n<prerequisitesChosen>Counting items into categories</prerequisitesChosen>\n<prerequisitesChosen>Converting frequencies to percentages</prerequisitesChosen>\n<situation>You are a junior market researcher summarising survey votes for favourite sports.</situation>\n<easy>\n<picked_skill>Basic addition of whole numbers</picked_skill>\n<questionReasoning>Single-step addition of three whole numbers; low floor; uses KaTeX.</questionReasoning>\n<question>The survey shows $12$ votes for basketball, $9$ for soccer and $5$ for swimming. Total responses?</question>\n<answerReasoning>Add $12+9+5=26$.</answerReasoning>\n<answer>$26$</answer>\n</easy>\n<medium>\n<picked_skill>Counting items into categories</picked_skill>\n<questionReasoning>Two-step counting of occurrences in a short list; builds on easy.</questionReasoning>\n<question>Responses: basketball, soccer, basketball, swimming, soccer, basketball, swimming, soccer. How many chose soccer?</question>\n<answerReasoning>Soccer appears $3$ times in the list.</answerReasoning>\n<answer>$3$</answer>\n</medium>\n<hard>\n<picked_skill>Converting frequencies to percentages</picked_skill>\n<questionReasoning>Multi-step: sum frequencies then compute percentage; combines addition and division.</questionReasoning>\n<question>Basketball $15$ votes, soccer $10$, swimming $5$. What percent chose swimming?</question>\n<answerReasoning>Total $15+10+5=30$. Swimming $5/30=1/6$, percent $=16.\\overline{6}\\%$.</answerReasoning>\n<answer>$16.\\overline{6}\\ \\%$</answer>\n</hard>\n</root>","PreviousLessonSkills":"","promptLogID":"01KANZNC3Q6CGDTWAEE23VA34C","SubtopicID":"subtopic_01GTC0BPPFC7SK14KHJW3Y93PB","GradeLevel_3To6":"false","LessonID":"01KANZMKP8HY2MS8001EKHDX3V","maxCompletionTokens":"16384","baseURL":"https://api.cerebras.ai/v1","LessonPlanID":"lp_01KANZMECCDYDZQSYAW3MCNN4D","RelevantExamples":"Easy examples:\n1. [EASY] Why does a frequency table help organise data into categories?\n   Answer: A frequency table helps organise data into categories by grouping values and tallying their occurrences.\n\n2. [EASY] How do you know each row in a frequency table represents the count of each category?\n   Answer: Each row shows how many times that category appears in the data. The frequency is the count of occurrences.\n\n\nMedium examples:\n1. [MEDIUM] Explain why frequency tables help to understand data distribution across categories.\n   Answer: Frequency tables show how data is spread across categories, making patterns and concentrations visible.\n\n\nHard examples:\n1. [HARD] Why is organising data into frequency tables useful for solving real-world problems like surveys?\n   Answer: Organising data into frequency tables is useful for solving real-world problems like surveys by highlighting key trends and totals.\n\n2. [HARD] How do you know the total frequency in a frequency distribution table equals the sum of the counts in all categories?\n   Answer: The table divides all data into separate categories. Each data point fits into exactly one category. Adding the counts from all categories must give the total number of data points.\n","model":"gpt-oss-120b","provider":"cerebras","IsFirstPeriod":"true","GradeLevel_PrepTo2":"false","NodeID":"01KANZMKP96DR8GNRTWPKXNXEK","FirstSkill":"Analysing and creating frequency tables with categorical data","Duration":"0.00 minutes","system":"\n\n# ROLE\nYou are a Grade 8 maths teacher in Australia. Your task is to create an engaging real-world problem-solving scenario with three questions that assess foundational prerequisite skills for the {Skill}.\nThe {Skill} is the first skill to be taught in the lesson, so we must prepare the students' foundational knowledge for this skill.\n\n# GUIDELINES\n\n1. Identify Pre-requisite Skills:\n   - List exactly $3$ prerequisite skills required before learning {Skill}. These MUST be skills taught earlier (typically prior year or many lessons earlier), appropriate to Grade 8.\n   - These are assumed foundational skills; do **not** include the target {Skill} themselves or any skills that are too advanced.\n   - For example, if teaching probability (Grade 9), valid prerequisites might be:\n     * Decimal operations\n     * Fraction equivalence\n     * Ratio understanding\n   - Invalid prerequisites would be:\n     * Skills being taught in the current lesson\n     * Skills that are too advanced\n   - Keep this list concise to minimise context length.\n\n2. Target and Assessment Focus:\n   - Focus on assessing the identified prerequisite skills, not the current {Skill}\n   - Ensure the role would naturally use the prerequisite skills in their work\n   - STRICTLY focus on assessing the prerequisite skills only - do not introduce the current [Skill] or unrelated concepts\n   - Test ONLY the prerequisite skills - do not add additional mathematical concepts\n\n3. Difficulty and Skill Mapping (DO NOT assign prerequisite skills to difficulty levels based on their order in the list):\n   - Easy: Choose the most foundational prerequisite skill that other skills build upon.\n   - Medium: Choose a prerequisite skill that naturally builds upon the easy skill or combines basic concepts.\n   - Hard: Choose a prerequisite skill that either extends previous skills or combines multiple simpler skills in a meaningful way.\n   - Each question should target a different prerequisite skill or application\n\n4. Question Design Planning (questionReasoning) - Prerequisite Focus:\n   - Verifies the mathematical approach is correct and prerequisite to {Skill}\n   - Ensures a clear low-floor entry point without relying on unrelated skills\n\n\n# INSTRUCTIONS\n\n1. Create an Immersive Role and Situation:\n\t - Put students in a specific professional or real-world role related to {Context}\n   - Create an urgent or engaging situation where they need to make decisions\n   - Make the stakes feel real and relevant to their role\n   - Help them imagine themselves actually doing this job\n   - Ensure the role would naturally use the prerequisite skills in their work\n   - Make the situation specific enough that multiple related questions arise naturally\n   - Consider roles like: engineer, scientist, analyst, designer, planner, etc.\n\n2. Write three connected questions that:\n   - All stem from the same core situation\n   - Use only factually accurate information about the domain/context (e.g., correct rules for sports, accurate professional practices, realistic scenarios)\n   - Make questions personal and immersive:\n     * Use phrases like \"How would you...\", \"How much do you need...\", \"What should you...\"\n     * Put the student in an active problem-solving role\n     * Frame questions as real choices they need to make\n     * Make them feel like the expert solving the problem\n     * Avoid theoretical questions that break immersion\n   - Progress naturally in difficulty:\n     * Easy: Solvable by 100% of students. Single-step operations or straightforward reasoning.\n     * Medium: Solvable by 70% of students. Two-step operations or slightly abstract reasoning.\n     * Hard: Solvable by 50% of students. Multi-step operations or complex reasoning.\n   - Feel necessary to solve the real problem, not artificial\n   - Show how math helps achieve the goal\n   - Build upon each other when possible\n   - Ensure questions can be solved without visual aids\n   - Keep mathematical notation and concepts appropriate for Grade 8 level\n\n3. Question design planning (questionReasoning):\n   - Immediately before each \"question\" field, include a \"questionReasoning\" field with your forward-thinking plan that:\n     * Explains how the question aligns with the specified difficulty level (easy/medium/hard)\n     * Verifies the mathematical approach is correct\n     * Confirms compliance with formatting/KaTeX and length requirements\n     * Ensures a clear low-floor entry point without relying on unrelated skills\n\n4. Ensure high question quality:\n   - Avoid circular logic or self-answering questions (e.g. \"If X is true, is X true?\").\n   - Don't make the questions confusing or ambiguous.\n   - Ensure clear progression in complexity\n   - End questions with a question mark\n   - Avoid visuals like graphs or tables\n   - Have clear inputs and required outputs\n   - Include step-by-step solution reasoning\n   - Provide exact answers in KaTeX format\n   - Can be solved with the mathematical knowledge expected at Grade 8 level \n\n\n# WRITING\n- Write in Australia English grammar and spelling\n- Write at a Grade 8 Flesch-Kincaid reading level\n- Use simple language students will understand. Don't make it too wordy or complex.\n- Make questions grammatically and logically sound\n- Ensure scenarios are realistic and possible\n- Verify all context-specific facts are accurate (e.g., correct sport rules, accurate professional practices, realistic measurements)\n\n\n# CONCISENESS GUIDELINE:\n- Students are in school all day, impatient, and receiving large amounts of content. They dislike excessive reading during class.\n- Keep outputs EXTREMELY brief and focused — this directly supports attention, comprehension, and engagement for ALL learners.\n- For context questions: the situation provides context, so questions should be MINIMAL. Don't repeat scenario details in questions.\n- Cut ruthlessly: eliminate filler words, use imperatives, remove redundancy, and strip unnecessary context.\n- Transformation examples:\n  ❌ \"In order to find the total, we need to add the numbers from both groups together.\"\n  ✅ \"Add both groups to find the total.\"\n  ❌ \"Calculate what the result would be if you were to multiply...\"\n  ✅ \"Multiply...\"\n  ❌ \"Spiderman is tracking villains in two city districts and must record who appears where.\"\n  ✅ \"Which villains appear in both districts?\"\n- Every single word must earn its place. If a word can be removed without losing mathematical clarity, REMOVE IT.\n- ⚠️ Maintain grammar: Never over-compress to nonsense like \"Add groups.\" or \"Total?\". Questions must be complete and grammatically correct.\n\n\n## GRADE LEVEL GUIDELINES (Grades 7–10)\n- Language: Formal and precise; multiple sentences allowed (max 3 sentences if needed).\n- Vocabulary: Consistent use of mathematical terminology (coefficient, gradient, factorise). Define once if nonstandard.\n- Question Length: Concise but complete; can include conditions/constraints.\n- Representation: Primarily abstract/symbolic (algebra, functions, probability).\n- Numbers: Integers (incl. negatives), fractions/decimals, surds (as appropriate), ratios/rates, algebraic expressions.\n- Operations allowed Grade 7-10: Multi-step procedures, equation solving\n- Operations allowed Grade 8+: factorising, proportional reasoning, index laws, Pythagoras/trig set-ups, statistical reasoning.\n- Grid References: When referencing grid positions, use letter-number format (e.g. A1, B2) unless coordinates are explicitly required.\n- Difficulty:\n  * Enabling/Easy = Accessible entry (single symbolic step or direct substitution) that sets up the main idea.\n  * Medium = Procedural multi-step (e.g., linear rule from a context, then evaluate).\n  * Hard = Multi-step reasoning in less familiar or abstract contexts; may require choosing a strategy or interpreting constraints.\n  * Extension = Deep conceptual insight/non-routine tasks: structure spotting, proof/justification, counterexample/edge-case analysis, or optimisation under constraints. Difficulty arises from structure, not messy numbers.\n\n\n\n# FORMATTING GUIDELINE\n- You will use the $ symbol for entering and exiting the KaTeX mode. For eg. $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Mathematical expressions and numbers within text should be wrapped in $ delimiters, but regular text should not be.\n- Remember to escape '%' when inside KaTeX mode, like '$\\%$', If you encounter '%' outside KaTeX mode wrap it with $\\%$ to confirm it to correct KaTeX.\n- Fraction Formatting: Always use '\\dfrac' instead of '\\frac' for all fractions to ensure they are displayed in more readable 'displaystyle' format.\n- ### CURRENCY FORMATTING\n  - **Dollar Values**: Always prefix monetary values with a dollar sign. The dollar sign must be escaped and the entire value wrapped in KaTeX.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$x$ has $\\$500$, and $y$ has $\\$300$ in bank'\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$\\$5', '$\\$10', '$\\$2.50'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$5', '5 dollars'\n  - **Cent Values**: For values less than a dollar, use whole numbers followed by \"c\" or \"cents\". Avoid using decimal notation like '$0.50'. This is critical for younger grade levels.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$50$c', '$50$ cents'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$0.50'\n- Whenever the context involves cost, price, or uses the word \"costing\", always prepend a dollar sign to the number and format it as $\\$<number>$ in KaTeX. For example: \"each costing $\\$7$\"\n  - **Context**: Apply these rules whenever the context involves money, cost, price, or value. For example: \"a coin worth $\\$2$\", \"an item costing $\\$7.50$\".\n- Make sure to prioritize writing text outside KaTeX mode, try to split up the section around the text. WRITE $x=4$ years $y=3$ NOT $x=4 \\text{years} y=3$.\n- Units should be written without delimiters and \\text function, please format according to the example below.\n  - eg input: \"$x$ is $5 \\text{m}$, and $y$ is only $2 \\text{cm}^2$ with angle $30^\\circ\"\n  - eg output: \"$x$ is $5$ m, and $y$ is only $2$ cm$^2$ with angle $30^\\circ$\"\n- ### Missing Digit Problems\n  - **CRITICAL:** For fill-in-the-blank or missing digit questions, ONLY use '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$' format. NEVER use \"_\", \"__\", \"___\", or any other blank format.\n  - ALWAYS wrap '\\underline{\\phantom{0}}' in $ delimiters: '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$'\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$' or '$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 + 58 = 676$'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '\\underline{\\phantom{0}}' (missing $ delimiters), '$7\\_4$', '$7__4$', '$7___4$'\n  - When outputting content regarding missing digits, follow this reasoning process in your reasoning field:\n    1. FIRST create a complete, valid arithmetic problem with all digits present. E.g. 'let's start with a valid equation like $734 - 58 = 676$'\n    2. Verify your arithmetic is correct\n    3. THEN replace exactly one digit with '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$'. E.g. write '$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 + 58 = 676$' to show '7_4 + 58 = 676'.\n    4. Ensure that exactly one digit (0-9) makes the equation true\n    5. If multiple digits work or no digit works, discard and try again\n  - Complete reasoning: \"lets start with a valid equation $734 - 58 = 676$. This is correct. Then, lets remove one digit to create $7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 - 58 = 676$ where blank must be 3.\"\n- Use KaTeX from simple numbers to expressions to all things math that should be in KaTeX, except text, prioritize text outside the KaTeX mode.\n- For numbers $1\\,000$ and above, use thin spaces instead of commas as thousand separators (e.g. $1\\,234$, $12\\,345$, $123\\,456$) following international standards.\n- Do not use any HTML tags (like <b>, <i>, <strong>, etc.) or markdown formatting (like **, *, _, etc.) in the text.\n- <EXTREMELY IMPORTANT> It is absolutely imperative that you MUST use KaTeX for mathematical expressions. Otherwise the Renderer will FAIL. </EXTREMELY IMPORTANT>\n\n\n<userExamples>\n# EXAMPLES\n## SKILL-SPECIFIC REFERENCE EXAMPLES\nBelow are curated examples that assess the final skills to be taught in the lesson.\nThese represent content that has been validated by our content team for accuracy, appropriate difficulty, and pedagogical effectiveness.\n\nEasy examples:\n1. [EASY] Why does a frequency table help organise data into categories?\n   Answer: A frequency table helps organise data into categories by grouping values and tallying their occurrences.\n\n2. [EASY] How do you know each row in a frequency table represents the count of each category?\n   Answer: Each row shows how many times that category appears in the data. The frequency is the count of occurrences.\n\n\nMedium examples:\n1. [MEDIUM] Explain why frequency tables help to understand data distribution across categories.\n   Answer: Frequency tables show how data is spread across categories, making patterns and concentrations visible.\n\n\nHard examples:\n1. [HARD] Why is organising data into frequency tables useful for solving real-world problems like surveys?\n   Answer: Organising data into frequency tables is useful for solving real-world problems like surveys by highlighting key trends and totals.\n\n2. [HARD] How do you know the total frequency in a frequency distribution table equals the sum of the counts in all categories?\n   Answer: The table divides all data into separate categories. Each data point fits into exactly one category. Adding the counts from all categories must give the total number of data points.\n\n\nEnsure warm up questions are prerequisites to these examples.\nUse these approved examples to guide the style and substance of your generated content.\n\n## IDEAL FORMAT EXAMPLES\nThe following examples show ideal structure, format, and writing style across different skills and contexts.\nUse them as templates for organization and quality standards.\n\nExample 1:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skills\": \"Unit conversion\",\n  \"Context\": \"Firefighting\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 10\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"prerequisitesChosen\": [\n    \"Basic division\",\n    \"Basic multiplication\", \n    \"Understanding ratios\"\n  ],\n  \"situation\": \"You're a firefighter managing water and equipment at a wildfire.\",\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic division\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will create a single-step calculation using time = volume ÷ rate with small, familiar numbers to ensure a low floor while targeting prerequisite division skills.\",\n    \"question\": \"How long to refill your fire truck's $5\\,000$ L tank from a hydrant supplying $80$ L/min?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Divide the tank volume by the flow rate: $5\\,000 \\div 80 = 62.5$ minutes.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$62.5$ minutes\"\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic multiplication\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will require students to use multi-step multiplication with larger numbers appropriate for Year 10 to calculate resource needs.\",\n    \"question\": \"Each firefighter carries $3$ water bottles. If $24$ teams of $8$ are deployed, how many bottles total?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"First find total firefighters: $24 \\times 8 = 192$ firefighters. Then multiply by bottles per person: $192 \\times 3 = 576$ water bottles.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$576$ water bottles\"\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Understanding ratios\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will use prerequisite ratio understanding in a multi-step context.\",\n    \"question\": \"Your team maintains a $3:1$ water to foam ratio. How many litres of concentrate for $120$ L water?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Using the ratio $3:1$, for every $3$ L of water, we need $1$ L of concentrate. So $120 \\div 3 = 40$ L of concentrate.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$40$\"\n  }\n}\n\nExample 2:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skills\": \"Matrix operations\",\n  \"Context\": \"AI\",\n  \"Country\": \"USA\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 12\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"prerequisitesChosen\": [\n    \"Basic arithmetic operations\",\n    \"Coordinate geometry\",\n    \"Solving simple linear equations\"\n  ],\n  \"situation\": \"You're an AI researcher developing computer vision for self-driving cars.\",\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic arithmetic operations\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will start with a multi-step arithmetic problem to calculate processing efficiency. This provides an accessible entry point for Year 12 students within the context of AI data processing.\",\n    \"question\": \"Your AI processes $51\\,200$ images per minute. If it runs for $15$ minutes, how many images are processed?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Multiply the rate by time: $51\\,200 \\times 15 = 768\\,000$ images.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$768\\,000$ images\"\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Solving simple linear equations\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will test the ability to solve a simple equation, which is a prerequisite for understanding matrix equations. This requires isolating a variable, a key algebraic skill.\",\n    \"question\": \"Your car's GPU power follows the equation $3x + 8 = 50$, where $x$ represents active cores. How many cores are currently active?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Solve for x: $3x = 50 - 8$, so $3x = 42$, which means $x = 14$ cores.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$14$\"\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Coordinate geometry\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will use the distance formula, which is a key concept in coordinate geometry. This question requires multiple steps making it more complex but still a foundational skill for understanding matrix transformations.\",\n    \"question\": \"Another car detected at sensor coordinates $(-15, 8)$ moves to $(9, 32)$. What distance did it travel?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Use distance formula: $d = \\sqrt{(9-(-15))^2 + (32-8)^2} = \\sqrt{24^2 + 24^2} = \\sqrt{576 + 576} = \\sqrt{1\\,152} = 24\\sqrt{2}$ units.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$24\\sqrt{2}$ units\"\n  }\n}\n\nExample 3:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skills\": \"Finding the area of composite shapes\",\n  \"Context\": \"Interior Design\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 5\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"prerequisitesChosen\": [\n    \"Area of rectangles\",\n    \"Basic multiplication\",\n    \"Understanding perimeter\"\n  ],\n  \"situation\": \"You're an interior designer planning flooring for a client's bedroom.\",\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Area of rectangles\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will start with a simple area of a rectangle calculation, as it's the most basic building block for this composite shape problem.\",\n    \"question\": \"A rectangular bedroom is $6$ m long and $4$ m wide. What is its area?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Area of a rectangle = length × width. So, $6 \\times 4 = 24$ square metres.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$24$ m$^2$\"\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic multiplication\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will test multiplication with slightly larger numbers in a design context to build toward area calculations.\",\n    \"question\": \"You need $12$ tiles per row and plan to use $8$ rows. How many tiles total?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Multiply tiles per row by number of rows: $12 \\times 8 = 96$ tiles.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$96$ tiles\"\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Understanding perimeter\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will test perimeter understanding, which is a key prerequisite skill for understanding the boundaries of composite shapes.\",\n    \"question\": \"A rectangular rug is $5$ m long and $3$ m wide. What is the perimeter?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Perimeter = $2 \\times (\\text{length} + \\text{width}) = 2 \\times (5 + 3) = 2 \\times 8 = 16$ m.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$16$ m\"\n  }\n}\n\nExample 4:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skills\": \"Introduction to Ratios\",\n  \"Context\": \"Cooking\",\n  \"Country\": \"UK\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 6\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"prerequisitesChosen\": [\n    \"Basic multiplication\",\n    \"Basic division\",\n    \"Understanding fractions\"\n  ],\n  \"situation\": \"You're a chef adjusting cookie recipe ingredients for different batch sizes.\",\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Understanding fractions\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will start with a simple fraction comparison in the context of ingredients, which is the most basic understanding needed for ratios.\",\n    \"question\": \"A recipe uses $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ cup sugar and $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ cup butter. Which ingredient is more?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Comparing the fractions, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is greater than $\\dfrac{1}{4}$, so there is more sugar.\",\n    \"answer\": \"Sugar\"\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic division\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will use a division problem to find a unit rate (amount per item), which is a core concept in ratios.\",\n    \"question\": \"$100$ chocolate chips for $20$ cookies. How many chips per cookie on average?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Divide the total number of chocolate chips by the number of cookies: $100 \\div 20 = 5$ chocolate chips per cookie.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$5$ chips\"\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic multiplication\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will test multiplication to scale up a recipe, which is a direct application of proportional thinking for understanding ratios.\",\n    \"question\": \"If $2$ cups of flour makes $12$ cookies, how many cups of flour do you need to make $24$ cookies?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"You are making double the number of cookies ($24 \\div 12 = 2$), so you need double the flour: $2 \\times 2 = 4$ cups.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$4$ cups\"\n  }\n}\n\nExample 5:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skills\": \"Vector analysis\",\n  \"Context\": \"Space Exploration\",\n  \"Country\": \"USA\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 12\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"prerequisitesChosen\": [\n    \"Coordinate geometry\",\n    \"Pythagorean theorem\",\n    \"Understanding direction and magnitude\"\n  ],\n  \"situation\": \"You're a mission control engineer guiding a spacecraft through Mars orbit.\",\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Coordinate geometry\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will start with coordinate calculations, which is fundamental for understanding vector components and positions in space.\",\n    \"question\": \"Your spacecraft moves from position $(12, -8)$ to $(20, 16)$. What are the changes in $x$ and $y$ coordinates?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Change in x: $20 - 12 = 8$. Change in y: $16 - (-8) = 24$. So $\\Delta x = 8$ and $\\Delta y = 24$.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$\\Delta x = 8$, $\\Delta y = 24$\"\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Pythagorean theorem\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will test the Pythagorean theorem, which is essential for calculating vector magnitudes and distances in 2D space.\",\n    \"question\": \"A thruster fires with $12$ N horizontal and $5$ N vertical force. What is total force magnitude?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Use Pythagorean theorem: $F = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13$ N.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$13$ N\"\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Understanding direction and magnitude\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will test understanding of how direction and magnitude work together, which is the core concept behind vectors before formal vector notation.\",\n    \"question\": \"Your spacecraft needs to travel $155$ km at a bearing of $045^\\circ$T. If you travel $105$ km, how much further to go?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Total distance needed is $155$ km. Distance already traveled is $105$ km. Remaining distance = $155 - 105 = 50$ km.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$50$ km\"\n  }\n}\n\nExample 6:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skills\": \"Identifying the edges of shapes\",\n  \"Context\": \"Sculptures\", \n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 4\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"prerequisitesChosen\": [\n    \"Counting sides of shapes\",\n    \"Recognising basic shapes\",\n    \"Basic addition\"\n  ],\n  \"situation\": \"You're a sculptor designing metal art pieces for a gallery.\",\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Counting sides of shapes\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will start with a simple counting question about the sides of a basic shape, as this is the most fundamental skill needed before identifying edges.\",\n    \"question\": \"You're cutting a triangular metal piece. How many sides does a triangle have?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"A triangle has $3$ sides.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$3$ sides\"\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Recognising basic shapes\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will ask students to identify a shape from its properties, building shape recognition skills.\",\n    \"question\": \"A client wants a shape with $4$ equal sides and $4$ corners. What shape should you make?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"A shape with $4$ equal sides and $4$ corners is a square.\",\n    \"answer\": \"Square\"\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic addition\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will combine counting sides of multiple shapes and adding them, which builds toward counting total edges in composite designs.\",\n    \"question\": \"You connect a triangle piece to a rectangle piece. How many sides do both pieces have altogether?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"A triangle has $3$ sides and a rectangle has $4$ sides. Total: $3 + 4 = 7$ sides.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$7$ sides\"\n  }\n}\n\nExample 7:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skills\": \"Calculating the angle sum of a quadrilateral\",\n  \"Context\": \"Smart Cities\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\", \n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 7\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"prerequisitesChosen\": [\n    \"Angles in triangles\",\n    \"Understanding interior angles\",\n    \"Basic angle addition\"\n  ],\n  \"situation\": \"You're an urban planner designing traffic systems for a smart city.\",\n  \"easy\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Angles in triangles\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I will start with triangle angle sum, which is the foundational concept for understanding angle sums in polygons, as quadrilaterals can be divided into triangles.\",\n    \"question\": \"A triangular park has two angles measuring $45^\\circ$ and $75^\\circ$. What is the third angle?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Angles in a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$: $180^\\circ - 45^\\circ - 75^\\circ = 60^\\circ$.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$60^\\circ$\"\n  },\n  \"medium\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Understanding interior angles\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will test understanding of interior angles, which directly builds toward understanding angles in quadrilaterals.\",\n    \"question\": \"A sensor monitors an angle of $140^\\circ$. What type of angle is this?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"An angle of $140^\\circ$ is greater than $90^\\circ$, so it is an obtuse angle.\",\n    \"answer\": \"Obtuse\"\n  },\n  \"hard\": {\n    \"picked_skill\": \"Basic angle addition\",\n    \"questionReasoning\": \"I have to make sure it's harder than the previous question. I will test angle addition skills, which students need to add up the angles in a quadrilateral.\",\n    \"question\": \"Four smart streetlights light up a rectangular intersection. Each streetlight has $90^\\circ$ coverage. What's the total coverage angle?\",\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Each corner has $90^\\circ$ coverage. Sum of all four corners: $90^\\circ + 90^\\circ + 90^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 360^\\circ$.\",\n    \"answer\": \"$360^\\circ$\"\n  }\n}\n\n","apiCallDurationMs":"1937.67","isReasoningModel":"true","ContextDetails":"","modelType":"reasoning","contextWindow":"131072","output":"{\"prerequisitesChosen\":[\"Basic addition of whole numbers\",\"Counting items into categories\",\"Converting frequencies to percentages\"],\"situation\":\"You are a junior market researcher summarising survey votes for favourite sports.\",\"easy\":{\"picked_skill\":\"Basic addition of whole numbers\",\"questionReasoning\":\"Single-step addition of three whole numbers; low floor; uses KaTeX.\",\"question\":\"The survey shows $12$ votes for basketball, $9$ for soccer and $5$ for swimming. Total responses?\",\"answerReasoning\":\"Add $12+9+5=26$.\",\"answer\":\"$26$\"},\"medium\":{\"picked_skill\":\"Counting items into categories\",\"questionReasoning\":\"Two-step counting of occurrences in a short list; builds on easy.\",\"question\":\"Responses: basketball, soccer, basketball, swimming, soccer, basketball, swimming, soccer. How many chose soccer?\",\"answerReasoning\":\"Soccer appears $3$ times in the list.\",\"answer\":\"$3$\"},\"hard\":{\"picked_skill\":\"Converting frequencies to percentages\",\"questionReasoning\":\"Multi-step: sum frequencies then compute percentage; combines addition and division.\",\"question\":\"Basketball $15$ votes, soccer $10$, swimming $5$. What percent chose swimming?\",\"answerReasoning\":\"Total $15+10+5=30$. Swimming $5/30=1/6$, percent $=16.\\\\overline{6}\\\\%$.\",\"answer\":\"$16.\\\\overline{6}\\\\ \\\\%$\"}}","Country":"Australia","ExistingContent":"","Strand":"Statistics","temperature":"0.000000","promptType":"lesson_plan","GradeLevel_7To10":"true"}
{"severity":"DEBUG","time":"2025-11-22T20:17:08+05:45","caller":"lesson_plan/node_reconciler.go:703","message":"[prompt-input-and-output]","traceID":"00000000000000000000000000000000","traceSampled":false,"requestID":"MacBook-Pro.local/fBn6sl4UuK-000001","userID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","authorizedUserID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","GradeLevel_7To10":"true","ExistingContent":"","IsFirstPeriod":"true","user":"\n# LESSON INPUTS\n- {Country} = Australia\n- {ClassYear} = Grade 8\n- {Context} = Flamingos\n- {Strand} = Statistics\n- {SubTopic} = Basic Data Representation\n- {Skill} = Analysing and creating frequency tables with categorical data\n\n## OUTPUT FORMAT\n- Use inline KaTeX for ANY numbers and expressions in All question and answer fields .eg year $2\\,020$, $15.6\\%$, $4$ cm$^2$, $x \\cdot b$.\n- Output strictly valid XML with the following format—output only the structured data and nothing else.\n- <EXTREMELY IMPORTANT> It is absolutely imperative that you MUST use KaTeX, Otherwise the Renderer will FAIL, ALWAYS use KaTeX in All questions and answer. </EXTREMELY IMPORTANT>\n\n<root>\n<title>Title that directly relates to the specific application</title>\n<example1Header>Situation</example1Header>\n<example1Body>Context description that sets up the professional application, max 160 characters</example1Body>\n<example2Header>Example</example2Header>\n<example2Reasoning>Brief introduction of the example followed by step-by-step mathematical reasoning, showing all calculations and verifying results, max 300 characters</example2Reasoning>\n<example2Body>Mathematical example showing how the skills are applied, max 160 characters</example2Body>\n</root>\n","modelType":"reasoning","Substrand":"Probability & Statistics Foundations","SubtopicID":"subtopic_01GTC0BPPFC7SK14KHJW3Y93PB","temperature":"0.000000","system":"\n# ROLE\nYou are a Math teacher creating a Real-World Application PowerPoint for a Grade 8 class in Australia. \nYour task is to create one in-depth, compelling real-world application related to the provided {SubTopic} and {Skill} within the {Context}.\nThe goal is to make it as engaging as possible in order to motivate students. We want the students to avoid the feeling of learning math without understanding why they're learning it.\n\n\n\n- Create an Immersive Application:\n   - Present a real-world professional context related to {Context}, through a \"Situation\" and an \"Example\"\n   - Describe the professional application in the \"Situation\" paragraph, based on the higher level {SubTopic} field, ensuring a natural connection to the {Context} without forcing the math\n   - Then, focus on a more concrete application in the \"Example\" paragraph, based on the {Skill} within the {Subtopic}\n   - Show how professionals in this field apply the {Skill} in the {SubTopic} in their daily life, making the application authentic and engaging\n   - Present the content in third person, from the viewpoint of professionals\n   - Ensure the application naturally incorporates the input skill and subtopic in their work\n   - Use simplified math that's accessible to students 2 years below Grade 8\n   - Avoid complex calculations or advanced applications of the skills\n   - Ensure the mathematics feels natural and necessary to the context, not forced or artificial\n   - Prioritize intuitive, practical applications where the math clearly solves a real problem\n   - Use everyday scenarios within the professional context that students can easily understand\n   - The application of the skill must be authentic to the profession. Avoid forcing a mathematical concept into a context where it isn't practically used.\n\n# INSTRUCTIONS\nUse the following step-by-step instructions to generate the output\n1. Identify the most compelling real-world scenario related to the {SubTopic} within the broader context of {Context}. Think through:\n- Does this scenario contain a compelling application?\n- Does the {Skill} and {SubTopic} naturally appear in this context?\n- Are students in Grade 8 going to be motivated and inspired by this scenario?\n2. For the selected scenario, craft a deeply engaging application:\n- The \"Situation\" paragraph, written in \"example1Body\", should focus on {SubTopic}, setting up the context, role, and application relating to {SubTopic}.\n- The \"Example\" paragraph, written in \"example2Body\", should focucs on the {Skill}, clearly demonstrate the mathematics behind the application, showing exactly how '{Skill}' is used to solve a real problem.\n- The mathematical problem should arise naturally from a practical need in the context. AVOID forcing mathematical concepts into scenarios where they don't realistically belong (e.g. a carpenter using 'partitioning' to measure wood is not a natural application).\n- Before writing the \"Example\" paragraph, first create detailed reasoning in \"example2Reasoning\", hidden from the user, that briefly states what example you'll create, then shows the step-by-step mathematical work for all calculations.\n- Start with \"In this example about [topic], we'll calculate...\" then provide concise reasoning.\n- Verify all mathematical results carefully, especially for statistical measures like mean, median, and mode.\n- Then, write the \"example2Body\" paragraph, clearly explaining what any variables or equations represent and how they are connected to the scenario.\n- Include the type of person/job that would apply the {SubTopic} and {Skill} and for what task.\n- Ensure the example demonstrates the practical, real-world benefit, such as improving a skill, solving a relatable problem, or supporting a future career.\n- Keep mathematical concepts simple and accessible to students 2 years below the target grade level\n- Use simple numbers that make calculations easy to follow\n- Avoid complex notation or multi-step operations unless absolutely necessary\n- Focus on the most basic and intuitive applications of the skills\n3. Review and reflect on your output. Make sure it follows all the guidelines and requirements. Adjust the outputs if necessary.\n4. Create a title that directly relates to the specific application (e.g., \"Water Conservation in Urban Planning\" instead of \"Algebra in Green Cities\").\n5. Display the output according the output structure specified.\n\n# WRITING GUIDELINE\n1. Write in simple language at a Grade 8 Flesch-Kincaid reading level in Australia.\n2. Use Australia English conventions\n3. Maintain grammatical and logical consistency\n4. State inputs and required outputs clearly\n5. Present all content in third person - avoid phrases like \"You are a\" or direct address to the student\n6. Make sure the maths clearly relates to the real-life example - don't confuse students with vague connections\n7. Use fewer technical terms and explain any that must be included\n8. Keep mathematics extremely simple - if a student 2 years below the target grade level cannot easily understand and follow the calculations, simplify further\n9. Prioritize practical necessity over mathematical sophistication - choose scenarios where basic math solves obvious, important problems\n10. Verify that the scenario is factually accurate and that the application of the skill is authentic to the real-world context (e.g. correct professional practices, realistic measurements).\n\n\n## GRADE LEVEL GUIDELINES (Grades 7–10)\n- Language: Formal and precise; multiple sentences allowed (max 3 sentences if needed).\n- Vocabulary: Consistent use of mathematical terminology (coefficient, gradient, factorise). Define once if nonstandard.\n- Question Length: Concise but complete; can include conditions/constraints.\n- Representation: Primarily abstract/symbolic (algebra, functions, probability).\n- Numbers: Integers (incl. negatives), fractions/decimals, surds (as appropriate), ratios/rates, algebraic expressions.\n- Operations allowed Grade 7-10: Multi-step procedures, equation solving\n- Operations allowed Grade 8+: factorising, proportional reasoning, index laws, Pythagoras/trig set-ups, statistical reasoning.\n- Grid References: When referencing grid positions, use letter-number format (e.g. A1, B2) unless coordinates are explicitly required.\n- Difficulty:\n  * Enabling/Easy = Accessible entry (single symbolic step or direct substitution) that sets up the main idea.\n  * Medium = Procedural multi-step (e.g., linear rule from a context, then evaluate).\n  * Hard = Multi-step reasoning in less familiar or abstract contexts; may require choosing a strategy or interpreting constraints.\n  * Extension = Deep conceptual insight/non-routine tasks: structure spotting, proof/justification, counterexample/edge-case analysis, or optimisation under constraints. Difficulty arises from structure, not messy numbers.\n\n<userExamples>\n# EXAMPLES\n## SKILL-SPECIFIC REFERENCE EXAMPLES\nBelow are curated examples from our approved question database that target the same skills. \n\n1. [MEDIUM] Explain why frequency tables help to understand data distribution across categories.\n   Answer: Frequency tables show how data is spread across categories, making patterns and concentrations visible.\n\n2. [EASY] How do you know a frequency table accurately represents data by counting each category separately?\n   Answer: By counting each category separately and ensuring every data point is counted exactly once, we maintain accuracy.\n\n\nUse these approved examples only to understand what the skills represent. These are not example outputs for your task.\n\n## IDEAL FORMAT EXAMPLES\nThe following examples show ideal structure, format, and writing style across different skills and contexts.\nUse them as templates for organization and quality standards.\n\nExample 1:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Solving problems involving ratios\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Ratios and Rates\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Number and Algebra\",\n  \"Context\": \"Cooking\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 5\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Ratios in Professional Cooking\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\", \n  \"example1Body\": \"Chefs scale recipes for different event sizes. A cake for $8$ people needs adjustment for larger groups.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about recipe scaling, we'll calculate the ratio between servings. From $8$ to $24$ servings: $24 \\div 8 = 3$. This means $3$ times the original amount for most ingredients. For flour: $3 \\times 1.5 = 4.5$ cups. For vanilla, a smaller ratio of $2$ might be used.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"Scaling from $8$ to $24$ servings: $24 \\div 8 = 3$. For $1.5$ cups flour, use $3 \\times 1.5 = 4.5$ cups. Vanilla doubles instead of triples.\"\n}\nExample 2:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Solving linear equations\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Linear Equation Basics\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Algebra\",\n  \"Context\": \"Green Cities\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\", \n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 8\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Water Usage Tracking in Green Cities\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"City water managers track daily usage to prevent shortages and maintain safe water levels.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about daily water tracking, we'll solve a simple equation. If a city uses $200$ litres per person daily, and there are $1\\,000$ people, total usage is $200 \\times 1\\,000 = 200\\,000$ litres. If the safe limit is $250\\,000$ litres, they have $250\\,000 - 200\\,000 = 50\\,000$ litres remaining as a safety buffer.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"With $1\\,000$ people using $200$ litres daily, total is $200 \\times 1\\,000 = 200\\,000$ litres. The $250\\,000$ limit leaves $50\\,000$ as buffer.\"\n}\nExample 3:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Identifying the dimensions of a matrix\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Matrix Foundations\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Algebra\",\n  \"Context\": \"Artificial Intelligence\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 10\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Matrices in Computer Vision\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"Computer vision engineers build AI systems that identify objects in photos using matrices of numbers.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\", \n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about computer vision, we'll analyze simple matrix dimensions. A $10 \\times 10$ pixel image contains $10 \\times 10 = 100$ elements. For basic image processing, engineers might use a $2 \\times 2$ section to analyze patterns across the image.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"A $10 \\times 10$ image has $100$ pixels. Engineers examine $2 \\times 2$ sections to detect patterns and identify shapes.\"\n}\nExample 4:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Finding angles using trigonometry\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Trigonometry Foundations\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Geometry\",\n  \"Context\": \"Skatepark Design\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 6\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Safety Angles in Skatepark Design\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"Skatepark designers ensure ramps have safe angles. Steep angles cause injuries; gentle angles create safer learning areas.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about skatepark safety, we'll determine safe ramp angles. A ramp at $90^\\circ$ (vertical) would cause serious injury. Beginner ramps use angles around $15^\\circ$ to $25^\\circ$. If a proposed ramp is $90^\\circ$, designers need to reduce it to $20^\\circ$ for safety, requiring a reduction of $90^\\circ - 20^\\circ = 70^\\circ$.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"A $90^\\circ$ ramp is dangerous. Designers reduce it to $20^\\circ$ for beginners: $90^\\circ - 20^\\circ = 70^\\circ$ reduction needed.\"\n}\nExample 5:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Comparing the metric and imperial systems\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Unit Conversion\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Measurement\",\n  \"Context\": \"The Himalayas\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 5\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Expedition Planning in the Himalayas\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"Sherpa guides convert between measurement systems when working with international teams.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about measurement conversion, we'll transform units for expedition planning. Converting $5\\,364$ metres to feet: $5\\,364 \\times 3.28 = 17\\,594$ feet. For temperature: $85°C$ to Fahrenheit using $F = (C \\times 9/5) + 32$: $(85 \\times 9/5) + 32 = 153 + 32 = 185°F$.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"For a $5\\,364$ metre peak, guides calculate $5\\,364 \\times 3.28 = 17\\,594$ feet. At $85°C$, they find $185°F$ using $F = (C \\times 9/5) + 32$.\"\n}\nExample 6:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Solving one-step algebraic equations involving addition and subtraction\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Solving Basic Equations\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Algebra\",\n  \"Context\": \"Cryptography\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 7\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Codes and Equations in Security\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"Security specialists use codes to protect information. Letters become numbers (A=$1$, B=$2$) in basic cryptography.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about decoding cryptographic messages, we'll solve simple equations. For the first letter: $x + 3 = 7$. Subtracting $3$ from both sides: $x = 4 =$ letter 'D'. For the second letter: $y - 2 = 6$. Adding $2$: $y = 8 =$ letter 'H'.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"Cryptographers decode by solving equations. For $x + 3 = 7$, $x = 4$ (letter 'D'). For $y - 2 = 6$, $y = 8$ (letter 'H').\"\n}\nExample 7:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Comparing likelihoods of events\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Likelihood\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Probability\",\n  \"Context\": \"Ocean Currents\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 8\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Wildlife Tracking with Probability\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"Marine biologists use probability to track endangered fish and place equipment in optimal locations.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about wildlife tracking, we'll apply probability to predict sightings. With probabilities of $0.65$ (warm currents) and $0.35$ (cold currents), we predict outcomes for $100$ observations. For warm currents: $100 \\times 0.65 = 65$ sightings. For cold: $100 \\times 0.35 = 35$ sightings.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"Tuna have $0.65$ probability in warm currents, $0.35$ in cold. In $100$ observations, expect $100 \\times 0.65 = 65$ in warm currents.\"\n}\nExample 8:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Counting money\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Money\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Statistics\",\n  \"Context\": \"Pottery Making\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 3\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Counting Sales in Pottery Business\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"Pottery shop owners count daily sales to track popular items and decide what to make next.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"In this example about pottery sales tracking, we'll use simple counting and addition. On Monday: $8$ bowls, $5$ cups, $3$ plates sold. Total items: $8 + 5 + 3 = 16$. Since bowls sold the most ($8$ out of $16$), the potter should make more bowls.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"On Monday, $8$ bowls, $5$ cups, $3$ plates sold. Total: $8 + 5 + 3 = 16$ items. Since bowls sold the most, the potter make more bowls.\"\n}\nExample 9:\nInput:\n{\n  \"Skill\": \"Solving long subtraction problems with missing digits\",\n  \"Subtopic\": \"Advanced Subtraction\",\n  \"Strand\": \"Statistics\",\n  \"Context\": \"Bullet Trains\",\n  \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n  \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 7\"\n}\nOutput:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Ticket Refund Errors in Bullet Train Systems\",\n  \"example1Header\": \"Situation\",\n  \"example1Body\": \"Bullet-train staff process ticket refunds and use subtraction to find missing digits in fares.\",\n  \"example2Header\": \"Example\",\n  \"example2Reasoning\": \"I'll start with a valid equation like $1\\,223 - 579 = 644$. This is correct. Then, I'll remove one digit to create $1\\,2\\underline{\\phantom{0}}3 - 579 = 644$, where the blank must be $2$. I will choose a thousands-level subtraction problem and frame it in a real-world context of train travel, requiring students to work backwards to find the missing digit.\",\n  \"example2Body\": \"Staff see $\\$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4-\\$286$ should equal $\\$494$. Checking shows $780-286=494$, so the missing digit is $8$.\"\n}\n</userExamples>\n\n\n\n# CONCISENESS GUIDELINE:\n- Students are in school all day, impatient, and receiving large amounts of content. They dislike excessive reading during class.\n- Keep outputs EXTREMELY brief and focused — this directly supports attention, comprehension, and engagement for ALL learners.\n- For context questions: the situation provides context, so questions should be MINIMAL. Don't repeat scenario details in questions.\n- Cut ruthlessly: eliminate filler words, use imperatives, remove redundancy, and strip unnecessary context.\n- Transformation examples:\n  ❌ \"In order to find the total, we need to add the numbers from both groups together.\"\n  ✅ \"Add both groups to find the total.\"\n  ❌ \"Calculate what the result would be if you were to multiply...\"\n  ✅ \"Multiply...\"\n  ❌ \"Spiderman is tracking villains in two city districts and must record who appears where.\"\n  ✅ \"Which villains appear in both districts?\"\n- Every single word must earn its place. If a word can be removed without losing mathematical clarity, REMOVE IT.\n- ⚠️ Maintain grammar: Never over-compress to nonsense like \"Add groups.\" or \"Total?\". Questions must be complete and grammatically correct.\n\n\n\n# FORMATTING GUIDELINE\n- You will use the $ symbol for entering and exiting the KaTeX mode. For eg. $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Mathematical expressions and numbers within text should be wrapped in $ delimiters, but regular text should not be.\n- Remember to escape '%' when inside KaTeX mode, like '$\\%$', If you encounter '%' outside KaTeX mode wrap it with $\\%$ to confirm it to correct KaTeX.\n- Fraction Formatting: Always use '\\dfrac' instead of '\\frac' for all fractions to ensure they are displayed in more readable 'displaystyle' format.\n- ### CURRENCY FORMATTING\n  - **Dollar Values**: Always prefix monetary values with a dollar sign. The dollar sign must be escaped and the entire value wrapped in KaTeX.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$x$ has $\\$500$, and $y$ has $\\$300$ in bank'\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$\\$5', '$\\$10', '$\\$2.50'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$5', '5 dollars'\n  - **Cent Values**: For values less than a dollar, use whole numbers followed by \"c\" or \"cents\". Avoid using decimal notation like '$0.50'. This is critical for younger grade levels.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$50$c', '$50$ cents'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$0.50'\n- Whenever the context involves cost, price, or uses the word \"costing\", always prepend a dollar sign to the number and format it as $\\$<number>$ in KaTeX. For example: \"each costing $\\$7$\"\n  - **Context**: Apply these rules whenever the context involves money, cost, price, or value. For example: \"a coin worth $\\$2$\", \"an item costing $\\$7.50$\".\n- Make sure to prioritize writing text outside KaTeX mode, try to split up the section around the text. WRITE $x=4$ years $y=3$ NOT $x=4 \\text{years} y=3$.\n- Units should be written without delimiters and \\text function, please format according to the example below.\n  - eg input: \"$x$ is $5 \\text{m}$, and $y$ is only $2 \\text{cm}^2$ with angle $30^\\circ\"\n  - eg output: \"$x$ is $5$ m, and $y$ is only $2$ cm$^2$ with angle $30^\\circ$\"\n- ### Missing Digit Problems\n  - **CRITICAL:** For fill-in-the-blank or missing digit questions, ONLY use '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$' format. NEVER use \"_\", \"__\", \"___\", or any other blank format.\n  - ALWAYS wrap '\\underline{\\phantom{0}}' in $ delimiters: '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$'\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$' or '$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 + 58 = 676$'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '\\underline{\\phantom{0}}' (missing $ delimiters), '$7\\_4$', '$7__4$', '$7___4$'\n  - When outputting content regarding missing digits, follow this reasoning process in your reasoning field:\n    1. FIRST create a complete, valid arithmetic problem with all digits present. E.g. 'let's start with a valid equation like $734 - 58 = 676$'\n    2. Verify your arithmetic is correct\n    3. THEN replace exactly one digit with '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$'. E.g. write '$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 + 58 = 676$' to show '7_4 + 58 = 676'.\n    4. Ensure that exactly one digit (0-9) makes the equation true\n    5. If multiple digits work or no digit works, discard and try again\n  - Complete reasoning: \"lets start with a valid equation $734 - 58 = 676$. This is correct. Then, lets remove one digit to create $7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 - 58 = 676$ where blank must be 3.\"\n- Use KaTeX from simple numbers to expressions to all things math that should be in KaTeX, except text, prioritize text outside the KaTeX mode.\n- For numbers $1\\,000$ and above, use thin spaces instead of commas as thousand separators (e.g. $1\\,234$, $12\\,345$, $123\\,456$) following international standards.\n- Do not use any HTML tags (like <b>, <i>, <strong>, etc.) or markdown formatting (like **, *, _, etc.) in the text.\n- <EXTREMELY IMPORTANT> It is absolutely imperative that you MUST use KaTeX for mathematical expressions. Otherwise the Renderer will FAIL. </EXTREMELY IMPORTANT>\n","maxTokens":"0","maxCompletionTokens":"16384","promptType":"lesson_plan","PreviousLessonSkills":"","Strand":"Statistics","ContextDetails":"","baseURL":"https://api.cerebras.ai/v1","model":"gpt-oss-120b","isReasoningModel":"true","GradeLevel_3To6":"false","Context":"Flamingos","YearOrder":"8","Country":"Australia","ContentDescriptor":"","provider":"cerebras","contextWindow":"131072","nodeType":"*lessonplan.ApplicationNode","ClassYear":"Grade 8","FirstSubstrand":"Basic Data Representation","architecture":"MoE","inferenceSpeed":"3000-tokens-per-sec","GradeLevel_PrepTo2":"false","LessonPlanID":"lp_01KANZMECCDYDZQSYAW3MCNN4D","Skills":"Analysing and creating frequency tables with categorical data","Duration":"0.00 minutes","RelevantExamples":"1. [MEDIUM] Explain why frequency tables help to understand data distribution across categories.\n   Answer: Frequency tables show how data is spread across categories, making patterns and concentrations visible.\n\n2. [EASY] How do you know a frequency table accurately represents data by counting each category separately?\n   Answer: By counting each category separately and ensuring every data point is counted exactly once, we maintain accuracy.\n","Blueprint":"","NodeID":"01KANZMKPAMETXRDT1PMTEBVD2","FirstSkill":"Analysing and creating frequency tables with categorical data","rawOutput":"<root>\n<title>Flamingo Colour Counts for Conservation</title>\n<example1Header>Situation</example1Header>\n<example1Body>A wildlife biologist records the colour of flamingos spotted at a wetland to plan protection measures.</example1Body>\n<example2Header>Example</example2Header>\n<example2Reasoning>In this example about flamingo sightings, we count birds per colour: Pink $12$, White $5$, Grey $3$. Adding gives total $20$. A frequency table lists each colour with its count, showing which colour is most common.</example2Reasoning>\n<example2Body>Frequency table: Pink $12$, White $5$, Grey $3$ (total $20$). Pink is most frequent.</example2Body>\n</root>","output":"{\"title\":\"Flamingo Colour Counts for Conservation\",\"example1Header\":\"Situation\",\"example1Body\":\"A wildlife biologist records the colour of flamingos spotted at a wetland to plan protection measures.\",\"example2Header\":\"Example\",\"example2Reasoning\":\"In this example about flamingo sightings, we count birds per colour: Pink $12$, White $5$, Grey $3$. Adding gives total $20$. A frequency table lists each colour with its count, showing which colour is most common.\",\"example2Body\":\"Frequency table: Pink $12$, White $5$, Grey $3$ (total $20$). Pink is most frequent.\"}","promptLogID":"01KANZNDWYX9P7D9PS0JQ18QNS","LessonID":"01KANZMKP8HY2MS8001EKHDX3V","apiCallDurationMs":"1693.83"}
