tag per corrected string (repeated tags), e.g.:\n<root>\n<correctedFields>x = 5</correctedFields>\n<correctedFields>y = 2cm^2</correctedFields>\n<correctedFields>z = \\frac{a}{b}</correctedFields>\n</root>\n\n# input\n[{\"errorDetail\":[\"Undelimited KaTeX found in word `5th` at character position 24\\nContext: `researcher picks every 5th name from`\\nError:\\nnumber found in `5th`\",\"Undelimited KaTeX found in word `100` at character position 55\\nContext: `alphabetical list of 100 students to`\\nError:\\nnumber found in `100`\"],\"field\":\"A researcher picks every 5th name from an alphabetical list of 100 students to interview. What sampling method is this?\"}]","contextWindow":"131072"}
{"severity":"DEBUG","time":"2025-11-22T20:18:54+05:45","caller":"lesson_plan/node_reconciler.go:703","message":"[prompt-input-and-output]","traceID":"00000000000000000000000000000000","traceSampled":false,"requestID":"MacBook-Pro.local/fBn6sl4UuK-000002","userID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","authorizedUserID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","LessonID":"lsn_01KANZQZV0GDGC33CBF31PH7RT","SectionID":"wsec_01KANZQZV0GDGC33CBFQDCJPJJ","user":"\n# LESSON INPUTS\n- Skill: Identifying different sampling methods\n- Country: Australia\n- ClassYear: Grade 8\n- Question Count: 2\n- Difficulty Mapping: 1: hard, 2: easy\n\n# OUTPUT INSTRUCTIONS\n- Produce exactly 2 multiple choice questions.\n- For each question_i, follow the difficulty level specified in the Difficulty Mapping.\n- Use the Difficulty Mapping to determine which difficulty level to apply to each question number.\n\n# OUTPUT SCHEMA\n<root>\n<question_1>\n<difficulty>difficulty level: 'enabling', 'easy', 'medium', 'hard', or 'extension'</difficulty>\n<question>question [ max $160$ characters ]</question>\n<answerOptions>answer option 1 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 2 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 3 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 4 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerReasoning>answer reasoning [ max $240$ characters ]</answerReasoning>\n<answer>answer [ max $40$ characters ]</answer>\n</question_1>\n<question_2>\n<difficulty>difficulty level: 'enabling', 'easy', 'medium', 'hard', or 'extension'</difficulty>\n<question>question [ max $160$ characters ]</question>\n<answerOptions>answer option 1 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 2 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 3 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 4 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerReasoning>answer reasoning [ max $240$ characters ]</answerReasoning>\n<answer>answer [ max $40$ characters ]</answer>\n</question_2>\n...\n<question_2>\n<difficulty>difficulty level: 'enabling', 'easy', 'medium', 'hard', or 'extension'</difficulty>\n<question>question [ max $160$ characters ]</question>\n<answerOptions>answer option 1 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 2 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 3 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>answer option 4 [ STRICT max $50$ characters ]</answerOptions>\n<answerReasoning>answer reasoning [ max $240$ characters ]</answerReasoning>\n<answer>answer [ max $40$ characters ]</answer>\n</question_2>\n</root>\n","isReasoningModel":"true","promptLogID":"01KANZRMRGRF6ZW1386MSSJKNZ","GradeLevel_7To10":"true","IsFirstPeriod":"true","model":"gpt-oss-120b","contextWindow":"131072","output":"{\"question_1\":{\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question\":\"A school surveys student opinions on a new lunch menu. It randomly selects three whole year‑level classes and asks every student in those classes to answer. Which sampling method is used?\",\"answerOptions\":[\"Cluster sampling\",\"Stratified sampling\",\"Simple random sampling\",\"Systematic sampling\"],\"answerReasoning\":\"Selecting entire classes (clusters) at random and surveying all members of those classes is a cluster sample.\",\"answer\":\"Cluster sampling\"},\"question_2\":{\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question\":\"A researcher picks every 5th name from an alphabetical list of 100 students to interview. What sampling method is this?\",\"answerOptions\":[\"Simple random sampling\",\"Systematic sampling\",\"Cluster sampling\",\"Convenience sampling\"],\"answerReasoning\":\"Choosing every 5th person follows a fixed interval, which defines systematic sampling.\",\"answer\":\"Systematic sampling\"}}","LessonPlanID":"wp_01KANZQNYJF89MQFWPHHX80K0Y","PreviousLessonSkills":"","Skills":"Identifying different sampling methods","ClassYear":"Grade 8","modelType":"reasoning","Duration":"0.00 minutes","ExistingContent":"","MultipleChoiceQuestionNodeDifficulty_extension":"false","MultipleChoiceQuestionNodeDifficulty_hard":"true","MultipleChoiceQuestionNodeDifficulty_enabling":"false","ContextDetails":"","baseURL":"https://api.cerebras.ai/v1","nodeType":"*lessonplan.MultipleChoiceQuestionNode","NodeID":"wpn_01KANZQZV0GDGC33CBG0EH58VQ","GradeLevel_3To6":"false","maxCompletionTokens":"32768","provider":"cerebras","rawOutput":"<root>\n<question_1>\n<difficulty>hard</difficulty>\n<question>A school surveys student opinions on a new lunch menu. It randomly selects three whole year‑level classes and asks every student in those classes to answer. Which sampling method is used?</question>\n<answerOptions>Cluster sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>Stratified sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>Simple random sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>Systematic sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerReasoning>Selecting entire classes (clusters) at random and surveying all members of those classes is a cluster sample.</answerReasoning>\n<answer>Cluster sampling</answer>\n</question_1>\n<question_2>\n<difficulty>easy</difficulty>\n<question>A researcher picks every 5th name from an alphabetical list of 100 students to interview. What sampling method is this?</question>\n<answerOptions>Simple random sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>Systematic sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>Cluster sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerOptions>Convenience sampling</answerOptions>\n<answerReasoning>Choosing every 5th person follows a fixed interval, which defines systematic sampling.</answerReasoning>\n<answer>Systematic sampling</answer>\n</question_2>\n</root>","apiCallDurationMs":"1477.53","MultipleChoiceQuestionNodeDifficulty_easy":"true","FirstSubstrand":"Basic Data Representation","Substrand":"Sampling","ContentDescriptor":"","YearOrder":"8","Context":"","RelevantExamples":"1. [HARD] Fill in the blank:\nA team of talented maths students being selected to represent a school in an interschool maths competition is an example of $[?]$.\n   Options:\n   ● Judgmental sampling\n   ○ Random sampling\n   ○ Systematic sampling\n   ○ Convenience sampling\n\n2. [HARD] A university surveys students about campus facilities by randomly selecting $10$ classes and surveying every student in those classes. \nWhat sampling method is used, and what is a potential issue?\n   Options:\n   ○ Simple random sampling; too small a sample\n   ○ Stratified sampling; unequal group sizes\n   ○ Systematic sampling; biased selection process\n   ● Cluster sampling; unrepresentative clusters\n\n3. [HARD] Dr. Lee conducted a medical study on $500$ patients in a diverse metropolitan area. Later, he repeated the study with $100$ patients in a homogeneous rural community.\nCompared to the original study, the results of the second study would be:\n   Options:\n   ○ Equally biased\n   ○ More reliable\n   ● More biased\n   ○ Less biased\n\n4. [HARD] A sports association plans to survey members from all its clubs, ensuring that each club is represented in proportion to its size.\nWhich sampling method should be used?\n   Options:\n   ○ Quota sampling\n   ● Stratified sampling\n   ○ Systematic sampling\n   ○ Cluster sampling\n\n5. [HARD] A professor conducted a study of all university students but only sampled $5$ students, leading to bias.\nHow can the professor reduce bias in future studies?\n   Options:\n   ○ Ask different types of questions\n   ○ Use convenience sampling\n   ○ Select a smaller, targeted group\n   ● Increase the sample size\n\n6. [HARD] One company surveys every household in $50$ randomly selected blocks. \nAnother divides voters by income and randomly selects $100$ from each group. \nWhat sampling methods are used?\n   Options:\n   ● Cluster; stratified\n   ○ Stratified; cluster\n   ○ Simple random; cluster\n   ○ Systematic; stratified\n\n7. [HARD] Ellen surveyed $50$ people in a library about their favourite book genre. The next day, she surveyed $10$ different people. \nCompared to the previous day, the results would be:\n   Options:\n   ○ More accurate\n   ● Less accurate\n   ○ Equally accurate\n   ○ More biased\n\n8. [HARD] A researcher wants a sample with $40\\%$ from urban areas, $30\\%$ from suburban areas, and $30\\%$ from rural areas. \nInterviewers find participants matching each group until the quotas are met. \nWhat type of sampling method is this?\n   Options:\n   ● Quota sampling\n   ○ Simple random sampling\n   ○ Cluster sampling\n   ○ Stratified sampling\n","temperature":"0.000000","promptType":"lesson_plan","Strand":"Statistics","DifficultyMapping":"1: hard, 2: easy","maxTokens":"0","inferenceSpeed":"3000-tokens-per-sec","Country":"Australia","FirstSkill":"Analysing and creating frequency tables with categorical data","system":"\n# ROLE\nYou are an expert mathematics educator creating high-quality multiple choice questions for a Grade 8 class in Australia.\nYour task is to generate multiple multiple choice questions in one go, with each question having its own difficulty level.\n\n# OVERVIEW\n- You will produce exactly 2 multiple choice questions.\n- The Difficulty mapping will map the question number to the difficulty level.\n- Each question contains four answer options with exactly one correct answer.\n\n# REQUIREMENTS\n- Each question must have exactly four answer options\n- All options must be plausible but only one must be correct\n- Each question must directly test the Skill being taught\n- Questions must be completely self-contained within the text - no external references\n- Do not include or reference any visual representations (diagrams, tables, graphs, or models)\n- Do not reference any external materials or props that would need teacher preparation\n- All necessary information must be explicitly stated in the question text\n- Each answer option must be 50 characters or fewer to fit within the user interface. Longer options will be cut off and render the question unusable.\n\n# INSTRUCTIONS (PER QUESTION)\n1. Create a clear and focused question that:\n    - Tests understanding of the Skill being taught\n    - Is appropriate for the specified grade level\n    - Provides all necessary information in the question text\n    - Does not rely on external materials or information not provided in the question\n    - Presents data or scenarios in a clear, text-based format\n2. Create four answer options/candidates that:\n    - Have plausible distractors that represent common misconceptions\n    - Are similar in length and structure\n    - Each option must be concise and fit within 50 characters maximum\n    - Prefer simple, direct answers over verbose explanations, abbreviated notations etc\n    - Avoid listing long sequences, sets, or expressions that exceed the character limit\n    - Do not include \"all of the above\" or \"none of the above\"\n    - Include exactly one correct answer, containing the exact answer text\n    - CRITICAL: Ensure LaTeX formatting is perfectly consistent between answerOptions and the answer field. If an option uses LaTeX (e.g., \"$Column 3$\"), the correct answer must use identical LaTeX formatting (e.g., \"$Column 3$\", NOT \"Column 3\" or \"$Column$ 3\"). If options are plain text, the answer should also be plain text.\n3. Vary the position of the correct answer:\n    - Distribute correct answers roughly equally across all four positions (position 1, 2, 3, and 4)\n    - Avoid patterns where the correct answer frequently appears in the same position\n\n## CRITICAL: LATEX CONSISTENCY EXAMPLES\nCORRECT LaTeX consistency:\n✅ \"answerOptions\": [\"Column $3$\", \"Column D\", \"Column $2$\", \"Column A\"], \"answer\": \"Column $2$\"\n✅ \"answerOptions\": [\"$90°$\", \"$60°$\", \"$120°$\", \"$180°$\"], \"answer\": \"$90°$\"\n✅ \"answerOptions\": [\"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4$\", \"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}5$\", \"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}6$\", \"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}7$\"], \"answer\": \"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}6$\"\n\nINCORRECT LaTeX inconsistency (THESE CAUSE SANITY CHECK FAILURES):\n❌ \"answerOptions\": [\"Column $3$\", \"Column D\", \"Column $2$\", \"Column A\"], \"answer\": \"Column 2\"\n❌ \"answerOptions\": [\"$3$\", \"$4$\", \"$5$\", \"$6$\"], \"answer\": \"3\"\n❌ \"answerOptions\": [\"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4$\", \"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}5$\", \"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}6$\", \"$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}7$\"], \"answer\": \"7_4\"\n\n## GOOD VS BAD EXAMPLES OF REGARDING VISUAL REPRESENTATIONS\nGood: \"A rectangle has a length of 6 cm and a width of 4 cm. What is its area?\"\nBad: \"Using the shape shown in the diagram, calculate its area.\"\n\nGood: \"The temperature starts at 20°C and increases by 5°C each hour. What is the temperature after 3 hours?\"\nBad: \"Using the thermometer provided by your teacher, record the temperature every hour and calculate the increase.\"\n\n# WRITING GUIDELINE\n- Write in Australia English grammar and spelling\n- Make questions grammatically and logically sound\n- Ensure scenarios are realistic and possible\n- When referencing grid positions, use letter-number format (e.g. A1, B2) unless coordinates are explicitly required\n\n\n# CONCISENESS GUIDELINE:\n- Students are in school all day, impatient, and receiving large amounts of content. They dislike excessive reading during class.\n- Keep outputs EXTREMELY brief and focused — this directly supports attention, comprehension, and engagement for ALL learners.\n- For context questions: the situation provides context, so questions should be MINIMAL. Don't repeat scenario details in questions.\n- Cut ruthlessly: eliminate filler words, use imperatives, remove redundancy, and strip unnecessary context.\n- Transformation examples:\n  ❌ \"In order to find the total, we need to add the numbers from both groups together.\"\n  ✅ \"Add both groups to find the total.\"\n  ❌ \"Calculate what the result would be if you were to multiply...\"\n  ✅ \"Multiply...\"\n  ❌ \"Spiderman is tracking villains in two city districts and must record who appears where.\"\n  ✅ \"Which villains appear in both districts?\"\n- Every single word must earn its place. If a word can be removed without losing mathematical clarity, REMOVE IT.\n- ⚠️ Maintain grammar: Never over-compress to nonsense like \"Add groups.\" or \"Total?\". Questions must be complete and grammatically correct.\n\n\n# FORMATTING GUIDELINE\n- **CRITICAL**: LaTeX formatting must be identical between answerOptions and answer fields to prevent sanity check failures\n\n\n# FORMATTING GUIDELINE\n- You will use the $ symbol for entering and exiting the KaTeX mode. For eg. $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Mathematical expressions and numbers within text should be wrapped in $ delimiters, but regular text should not be.\n- Remember to escape '%' when inside KaTeX mode, like '$\\%$', If you encounter '%' outside KaTeX mode wrap it with $\\%$ to confirm it to correct KaTeX.\n- Fraction Formatting: Always use '\\dfrac' instead of '\\frac' for all fractions to ensure they are displayed in more readable 'displaystyle' format.\n- ### CURRENCY FORMATTING\n  - **Dollar Values**: Always prefix monetary values with a dollar sign. The dollar sign must be escaped and the entire value wrapped in KaTeX.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$x$ has $\\$500$, and $y$ has $\\$300$ in bank'\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$\\$5', '$\\$10', '$\\$2.50'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$5', '5 dollars'\n  - **Cent Values**: For values less than a dollar, use whole numbers followed by \"c\" or \"cents\". Avoid using decimal notation like '$0.50'. This is critical for younger grade levels.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$50$c', '$50$ cents'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$0.50'\n- Whenever the context involves cost, price, or uses the word \"costing\", always prepend a dollar sign to the number and format it as $\\$<number>$ in KaTeX. For example: \"each costing $\\$7$\"\n  - **Context**: Apply these rules whenever the context involves money, cost, price, or value. For example: \"a coin worth $\\$2$\", \"an item costing $\\$7.50$\".\n- Make sure to prioritize writing text outside KaTeX mode, try to split up the section around the text. WRITE $x=4$ years $y=3$ NOT $x=4 \\text{years} y=3$.\n- Units should be written without delimiters and \\text function, please format according to the example below.\n  - eg input: \"$x$ is $5 \\text{m}$, and $y$ is only $2 \\text{cm}^2$ with angle $30^\\circ\"\n  - eg output: \"$x$ is $5$ m, and $y$ is only $2$ cm$^2$ with angle $30^\\circ$\"\n- ### Missing Digit Problems\n  - **CRITICAL:** For fill-in-the-blank or missing digit questions, ONLY use '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$' format. NEVER use \"_\", \"__\", \"___\", or any other blank format.\n  - ALWAYS wrap '\\underline{\\phantom{0}}' in $ delimiters: '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$'\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$' or '$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 + 58 = 676$'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '\\underline{\\phantom{0}}' (missing $ delimiters), '$7\\_4$', '$7__4$', '$7___4$'\n  - When outputting content regarding missing digits, follow this reasoning process in your reasoning field:\n    1. FIRST create a complete, valid arithmetic problem with all digits present. E.g. 'let's start with a valid equation like $734 - 58 = 676$'\n    2. Verify your arithmetic is correct\n    3. THEN replace exactly one digit with '$\\underline{\\phantom{0}}$'. E.g. write '$7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 + 58 = 676$' to show '7_4 + 58 = 676'.\n    4. Ensure that exactly one digit (0-9) makes the equation true\n    5. If multiple digits work or no digit works, discard and try again\n  - Complete reasoning: \"lets start with a valid equation $734 - 58 = 676$. This is correct. Then, lets remove one digit to create $7\\underline{\\phantom{0}}4 - 58 = 676$ where blank must be 3.\"\n- Use KaTeX from simple numbers to expressions to all things math that should be in KaTeX, except text, prioritize text outside the KaTeX mode.\n- For numbers $1\\,000$ and above, use thin spaces instead of commas as thousand separators (e.g. $1\\,234$, $12\\,345$, $123\\,456$) following international standards.\n- Do not use any HTML tags (like <b>, <i>, <strong>, etc.) or markdown formatting (like **, *, _, etc.) in the text.\n- <EXTREMELY IMPORTANT> It is absolutely imperative that you MUST use KaTeX for mathematical expressions. Otherwise the Renderer will FAIL. </EXTREMELY IMPORTANT>\n\n\n# DIFFICULTY GUIDELINES (Follow the one specified for each individual question)\n## Easy\n- Follow this guideline when the difficulty level is \"easy\"\n- The question should focus on a single-step operation or straightforward reasoning\n- Choose the most foundational prerequisite skill that other skills build upon\n- Ensure students need to perform at least one operation or make a basic deduction\n- Avoid directly stating the answer in the question\n- Characteristics of appropriate \"easy\" questions include:\n    - Single-step solutions\n    - Basic operations (+, -, ×, ÷) with friendly numbers\n    - Direct application of a single concept\n    - Clear, explicit language\n    - Familiar contexts\n    - Minimal abstraction\n    - Step-by-step guidance where needed\n    - Focus on foundational understanding\n## Hard\n- Follow this guideline when the difficulty level is \"hard\"\n- The question should involve multi-step operations, abstract reasoning, or complex values\n- Use an advanced prerequisite skill that challenges student understanding\n- Questions should require deep understanding and creative problem-solving\n- Characteristics of appropriate \"hard\" questions include:\n    - Multi-step solutions\n    - Complex operations and relationships\n    - Abstract reasoning required\n    - Application in unfamiliar contexts\n    - Strategic thinking and planning\n    - Limited guidance provided\n    - Integration of multiple concepts\n    - Challenging but not impossible\n\n## GRADE LEVEL GUIDELINES (Grades 7–10)\n- Language: Formal and precise; multiple sentences allowed (max 3 sentences if needed).\n- Vocabulary: Consistent use of mathematical terminology (coefficient, gradient, factorise). Define once if nonstandard.\n- Question Length: Concise but complete; can include conditions/constraints.\n- Representation: Primarily abstract/symbolic (algebra, functions, probability).\n- Numbers: Integers (incl. negatives), fractions/decimals, surds (as appropriate), ratios/rates, algebraic expressions.\n- Operations allowed Grade 7-10: Multi-step procedures, equation solving\n- Operations allowed Grade 8+: factorising, proportional reasoning, index laws, Pythagoras/trig set-ups, statistical reasoning.\n- Grid References: When referencing grid positions, use letter-number format (e.g. A1, B2) unless coordinates are explicitly required.\n- Difficulty:\n  * Enabling/Easy = Accessible entry (single symbolic step or direct substitution) that sets up the main idea.\n  * Medium = Procedural multi-step (e.g., linear rule from a context, then evaluate).\n  * Hard = Multi-step reasoning in less familiar or abstract contexts; may require choosing a strategy or interpreting constraints.\n  * Extension = Deep conceptual insight/non-routine tasks: structure spotting, proof/justification, counterexample/edge-case analysis, or optimisation under constraints. Difficulty arises from structure, not messy numbers.\n\n<userExamples>\n# EXAMPLES\n## SKILL-SPECIFIC REFERENCE EXAMPLES\nBelow are curated examples from our approved question database that target the same skills. \nThese represent content that has been validated by our content team for accuracy, appropriate difficulty, and pedagogical effectiveness.\n\n1. [HARD] Fill in the blank:\nA team of talented maths students being selected to represent a school in an interschool maths competition is an example of $[?]$.\n   Options:\n   ● Judgmental sampling\n   ○ Random sampling\n   ○ Systematic sampling\n   ○ Convenience sampling\n\n2. [HARD] A university surveys students about campus facilities by randomly selecting $10$ classes and surveying every student in those classes. \nWhat sampling method is used, and what is a potential issue?\n   Options:\n   ○ Simple random sampling; too small a sample\n   ○ Stratified sampling; unequal group sizes\n   ○ Systematic sampling; biased selection process\n   ● Cluster sampling; unrepresentative clusters\n\n3. [HARD] Dr. Lee conducted a medical study on $500$ patients in a diverse metropolitan area. Later, he repeated the study with $100$ patients in a homogeneous rural community.\nCompared to the original study, the results of the second study would be:\n   Options:\n   ○ Equally biased\n   ○ More reliable\n   ● More biased\n   ○ Less biased\n\n4. [HARD] A sports association plans to survey members from all its clubs, ensuring that each club is represented in proportion to its size.\nWhich sampling method should be used?\n   Options:\n   ○ Quota sampling\n   ● Stratified sampling\n   ○ Systematic sampling\n   ○ Cluster sampling\n\n5. [HARD] A professor conducted a study of all university students but only sampled $5$ students, leading to bias.\nHow can the professor reduce bias in future studies?\n   Options:\n   ○ Ask different types of questions\n   ○ Use convenience sampling\n   ○ Select a smaller, targeted group\n   ● Increase the sample size\n\n6. [HARD] One company surveys every household in $50$ randomly selected blocks. \nAnother divides voters by income and randomly selects $100$ from each group. \nWhat sampling methods are used?\n   Options:\n   ● Cluster; stratified\n   ○ Stratified; cluster\n   ○ Simple random; cluster\n   ○ Systematic; stratified\n\n7. [HARD] Ellen surveyed $50$ people in a library about their favourite book genre. The next day, she surveyed $10$ different people. \nCompared to the previous day, the results would be:\n   Options:\n   ○ More accurate\n   ● Less accurate\n   ○ Equally accurate\n   ○ More biased\n\n8. [HARD] A researcher wants a sample with $40\\%$ from urban areas, $30\\%$ from suburban areas, and $30\\%$ from rural areas. \nInterviewers find participants matching each group until the quotas are met. \nWhat type of sampling method is this?\n   Options:\n   ● Quota sampling\n   ○ Simple random sampling\n   ○ Cluster sampling\n   ○ Stratified sampling\n\n\nUse these approved examples to guide the style and substance of your generated content.\n\n## IDEAL FORMAT EXAMPLES\nThe following examples show ideal structure, format, and writing style across different skills and contexts.\nThey show the appropriate complexity required at each difficulty level requested.\nUse them as templates for organization and quality standards, and difficulty level requirements.\n\n<easy_examples>\n<example_1>\n<input>\n{\n    \"Skill\": \"Multiplication\",\n    \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n    \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 4\"\n}\n</input>\n<output>\n{\n    \"question\": \"What is $4 \\times 5$?\",\n    \"answerOptions\": [\"$15$\", \"$18$\", \"$20$\", \"$25$\"],\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"$4 \\times 5 = 20$\",\n    \"answer\": \"$20$\"\n}\n</output>\n</example_1>\n\n<example_2>\n<input>\n{\n    \"Skill\": \"Time\",\n    \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n    \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 3\"\n}\n</input>\n<output>\n{\n    \"question\": \"How many minutes in $1$ hour?\",\n    \"answerOptions\": [\"$30$\", \"$45$\", \"$100$\", \"$60$\"],\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"$1$ hour $= 60$ minutes\",\n    \"answer\": \"$60$\"\n}\n</output>\n</example_2>\n\n<example_3>\n<input>\n{\n    \"Skill\": \"Finding missing digits in subtraction\",\n    \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n    \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 4\"\n}\n</input>\n<output>\n{\n    \"question\": \"Find the missing digit: $5\\underline{\\phantom{0}} - 23 = 29$\",\n    \"answerOptions\": [\"$0$\", \"$1$\", \"$2$\", \"$3$\"],\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"$29 + 23 = 52$, so the missing digit is $2$\",\n    \"answer\": \"$2$\"\n}\n</output>\n</example_3>\n</easy_examples>\n<hard_examples>\n<example_1>\n<input>\n{\n    \"Skill\": \"Algebra\",\n    \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n    \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 9\"\n}\n</input>\n<output>\n{\n    \"question\": \"If $2x + 3y = 12$ and $3x - 2y = 1$, what is $x$?\",\n    \"answerOptions\": [\"$1$\", \"$3$\", \"$2$\", \"$4$\"],\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Elimination: multiply equations, solve for $x = 3$\",\n    \"answer\": \"$3$\"\n}\n</output>\n</example_1>\n\n<example_2>\n<input>\n{\n    \"Skill\": \"Trigonometry\",\n    \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n    \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 10\"\n}\n</input>\n<output>\n{\n    \"question\": \"If $\\sin A = \\dfrac{3}{5}$, what is $\\cos A$?\",\n    \"answerOptions\": [\"$\\dfrac{3}{4}$\", \"$\\dfrac{3}{5}$\", \"$\\dfrac{5}{3}$\", \"$\\dfrac{4}{5}$\"],\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"Use $\\sin^2 A + \\cos^2 A = 1$ to get $\\cos A = \\dfrac{4}{5}$\",\n    \"answer\": \"$\\dfrac{4}{5}$\"\n}\n</output>\n</example_2>\n\n<example_3>\n<input>\n{\n    \"Skill\": \"Finding missing digits in algebraic expressions\",\n    \"Country\": \"Australia\",\n    \"ClassYear\": \"Grade 9\"\n}\n</input>\n<output>\n{\n    \"question\": \"If $2\\underline{\\phantom{0}}7 + 358 = 645$, what digit makes this equation true?\",\n    \"answerOptions\": [\"$7$\", \"$8$\", \"$9$\", \"$6$\"],\n    \"answerReasoning\": \"$645 - 358 = 287$, so $2\\underline{\\phantom{0}}7 = 287$ means the digit is $8$\",\n    \"answer\": \"$8$\"\n}\n</output>\n</example_3>\n</hard_examples>\n","architecture":"MoE","GradeLevel_PrepTo2":"false","QuestionCount":"2","SubtopicID":"subtopic_01GTC0BHQKB2ZWJAA244FAWNEB","MultipleChoiceQuestionNodeDifficulty_medium":"false"}
