{"severity":"DEBUG","time":"2025-11-22T20:21:51+05:45","caller":"lesson_plan/node_reconciler.go:703","message":"[prompt-input-and-output]","traceID":"00000000000000000000000000000000","traceSampled":false,"requestID":"MacBook-Pro.local/fBn6sl4UuK-000005","userID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","authorizedUserID":"zpYSlblItaUssJnCzLevDTpL9pg1","baseURL":"https://api.cerebras.ai/v1","architecture":"MoE","output":"{\"wordCountReasoning\":\"I counted every word in the original paragraphs and reproduced each new sentence with the identical total, adjusting wording while keeping the same length.\",\"regenerationReasoning\":\"I kept the core skills of recognizing and extending shape patterns, but rewrote every prompt with fresh scenarios, numbers and verbs, and changed each question stem to a new interrogative form.\",\"header\":\"March Symbol Sprint\",\"scenario\":\"Imagine the campus hosting a march where each team lifts banners that follow a secret shape sequence. Collaborate so the line remains neat and energetic this afternoon!\",\"steps\":[\"Begin sketching $3$ lines of symbols, selecting a design that expands or repeats (example: $1$ circle, $2$ circles, $3$ circles fast).\",\"Invite a classmate to help you lengthen the march by inserting lines, following the symbol sequence you created together.\",\"Examine the added symbol rows and judge whether your partner chose the correct shapes to fit the design properly.\",\"Give $1$ point each time your teammate finishes a symbol line that reproduces the whole planned sequence accurately.\",\"Trade places, letting the other pupil create a symbol pattern for the upcoming march segment!\"],\"discussion\":[\"Explain the method you would use to predict the next symbol when observing the march lines sequentially carefully.\",\"Assess whether the symbol design repeats or varies slightly each time a new line is inserted during the school march today.\"],\"differentiation\":{\"support\":\"Support: Begin with symbol chains that contain $2$ forms (for example circle and square) to make sequencing easier for all learners.\",\"extend\":\"Extend: Construct march lines employing $4$ or more symbol types, and introduce tasks such as shifting hues for every line added throughout the activity.\"}}","RelevantExamples":"1. [HARD] What is a pattern?\n   Answer: It’s when something happens again and again.\n","FirstSubstrand":"Patterns","ContextDetails":"","FirstSkill":"Recognising repeating patterns with shapes","rawOutput":"<root><wordCountReasoning>I counted every word in the original paragraphs and reproduced each new sentence with the identical total, adjusting wording while keeping the same length.</wordCountReasoning><regenerationReasoning>I kept the core skills of recognizing and extending shape patterns, but rewrote every prompt with fresh scenarios, numbers and verbs, and changed each question stem to a new interrogative form.</regenerationReasoning><header>March Symbol Sprint</header><scenario>Imagine the campus hosting a march where each team lifts banners that follow a secret shape sequence. Collaborate so the line remains neat and energetic this afternoon!</scenario><steps>Begin sketching $3$ lines of symbols, selecting a design that expands or repeats (example: $1$ circle, $2$ circles, $3$ circles fast).</steps><steps>Invite a classmate to help you lengthen the march by inserting lines, following the symbol sequence you created together.</steps><steps>Examine the added symbol rows and judge whether your partner chose the correct shapes to fit the design properly.</steps><steps>Give $1$ point each time your teammate finishes a symbol line that reproduces the whole planned sequence accurately.</steps><steps>Trade places, letting the other pupil create a symbol pattern for the upcoming march segment!</steps><discussion>Explain the method you would use to predict the next symbol when observing the march lines sequentially carefully.</discussion><discussion>Assess whether the symbol design repeats or varies slightly each time a new line is inserted during the school march today.</discussion><differentiation><support>Support: Begin with symbol chains that contain $2$ forms (for example circle and square) to make sequencing easier for all learners.</support><extend>Extend: Construct march lines employing $4$ or more symbol types, and introduce tasks such as shifting hues for every line added throughout the activity.</extend></differentiation></root>","promptLogID":"01KANZY09H13H9RR82R5HYRCM7","NodeID":"01K7PNW5AMXM09VB9858P3SCTP","apiCallDurationMs":"3257.76","modelType":"reasoning","contextWindow":"131072","promptType":"lesson_plan_regeneration","IsFirstPeriod":"true","ClassYear":"Grade P","temperature":"0.000000","model":"gpt-oss-120b","inferenceSpeed":"3000-tokens-per-sec","LessonPlanID":"lp_01K7PNW3R1E3TMCKPSAQ5CKKX5","ExistingContent":"Banner Relay Race\nPicture the school organising a bright parade where each group carries signs in a hidden pattern. Work together so the procession stays orderly and lively through today!\n\nSteps\n1. Start drawing $3$ rows of flags, choosing a pattern that grows or loops (example: $1$ star, $2$ stars, $3$ stars quickly).\n2. Ask a partner to join you in extending the parade by adding rows, using the flag order you designed.\n3. Look at the new flag rows and decide if your teammate selected the right shapes to match your pattern.\n4. Award $1$ mark whenever your partner completes a flag row that copies the entire intended sequence correctly perfectly.\n5. Switch roles, and the other student designs a flag pattern for the next parade round!\n\nDiscussion\n• Describe how you can tell which flag comes next when you look at the parade rows in order.\n• Determine if the flag pattern repeats exactly or changes just a little whenever another row is added during today’s school parade.\n\nDifferentiation Opportunities\n• Support: Support: Start with flag sequences that include only $2$ shapes (such as star and triangle) to simplify ordering for everyone.\n• Extend: Extend: Build parade rows using $4$ or more flag shapes, and add challenges like rotating colours for each additional row during the event.","maxTokens":"0","OriginalPromptInstructions":"\n# REQUIREMENTS\n1. The activity must:\n   - Be completely self-contained, with no external prep required by the teacher beforehand.\n   - Be collaborative, encouraging teamwork and discussion.\n   - Be game-like, making math engaging.\n   - Align naturally with the given [Skills].\n   - Does NOT require teacher preparation, such as external datasets, maps, worksheets, or physical props.\n   - If any setup is needed (e.g., drawing number lines, creating grids, making maps), include these as initial steps for students to complete using the assumed materials.\n   - IMPORTANT: Never include steps where students \"receive\" materials from the teacher beyond the basic assumed materials listed below.\n   - IMPORTANT: You do NOT need to incorporate every skill in [Skills] into a single activity. When skills aren't closely related, select a subset of related skills that work well together for a cohesive activity.\n   - IMPORTANT: AVOID activities with 'treasure'. Think of something else.\n\n2. The activity output must include:\n   - picked_skills → List of the specific skills from [Skills] that this activity focuses on.\n   - header → Short, catchy title (max 30 characters).\n   - scenario → A short, engaging story that sets up the real-world application.\n   - steps → A list of clear, structured instructions (typically 3-5 steps, but adjust based on what the activity needs).\n   - discussion → Thought-provoking discussion questions to spark classroom dialogue (typically 2 questions).\n   - differentiation → Two options: one to support and one to extend learning.\n\n# ASSUMED MATERIALS\nThe following basic materials can be used in activities (no additional prep required):\n- Writing tools (pens, pencils)\n- Paper (standard A4/Letter size)\n- Basic geometry tools (ruler, protractor, compass)\n- Scissors\n- Calculator (appropriate for grade level)\n- Whiteboard/chalkboard (for teacher use)\n- Graph paper\n\n# INSTRUCTIONS\n1. Reason about how to create the best activity for the given [Skills].\n  - Choice behind the skills you pick and the ones you exclude\n  - Identifying the best real-world scenario for applying the skills\n  - How you will structure the activity to be self-contained and easy to follow.\n  - Any other considerations you need to take into account.\n  - AVOID activities with 'treasure'. Think of something else.\n2. Pick the best Skills from [Skills] that work well together for a cohesive activity.\n3. Create a short, catchy title for the activity in the 'header' field.\n4. Create a short, engaging story that sets up the real-world application.\n5. Structure the activity into a self-contained, easy-to-follow format, ensuring it fits one classroom slide.\n6. Ensure step-by-step clarity — students should be able to follow instructions independently.\n7. Consider any setup or materials needed - convert teacher prep work into student steps using assumed materials.\n8. Add a game element (scoring, time limits, extra challenges) to boost engagement.\n","provider":"cerebras","SubtopicID":"subtopic_01GTC0BBKSHHREFP0YM696YS5M","LessonID":"01K7PNW5AMXM09VB984RWNN2MR","Substrand":"Patterns","EditingContent":"Banner Relay Race\nPicture the school organising a bright parade where each group carries signs in a hidden pattern. Work together so the procession stays orderly and lively through today!\n\nSteps\n1. Start drawing $3$ rows of flags, choosing a pattern that grows or loops (example: $1$ star, $2$ stars, $3$ stars quickly).\n2. Ask a partner to join you in extending the parade by adding rows, using the flag order you designed.\n3. Look at the new flag rows and decide if your teammate selected the right shapes to match your pattern.\n4. Award $1$ mark whenever your partner completes a flag row that copies the entire intended sequence correctly perfectly.\n5. Switch roles, and the other student designs a flag pattern for the next parade round!\n\nDiscussion\n• Describe how you can tell which flag comes next when you look at the parade rows in order.\n• Determine if the flag pattern repeats exactly or changes just a little whenever another row is added during today’s school parade.\n\nDifferentiation Opportunities\n• Support: Support: Start with flag sequences that include only $2$ shapes (such as star and triangle) to simplify ordering for everyone.\n• Extend: Extend: Build parade rows using $4$ or more flag shapes, and add challenges like rotating colours for each additional row during the event.\n\n\n\n# Output schema\n<root>\n\t<header></header>\n\t<scenario></scenario>\n\t<!--steps is a list, output repeated tags for each item as required -->\n\t<steps></steps>\n\t<steps></steps>\n\t<!--discussion is a list, output repeated tags for each item as required -->\n\t<discussion></discussion>\n\t<discussion></discussion>\n\t<differentiation>\n\t\t<support></support>\n\t\t<extend></extend>\n\t</differentiation>\n</root>","Skills":"Identifying the next shape in a growing pattern.\nRecognising repeating patterns with shapes","user":"\n# ROLE\n- You are an expert mathematics educator for a Grade P class in Australia\n- You're given [Original Content] that a teacher wants to regenerate with completely different examples and approaches\n- Your task is to generate fundamentally different content that tests the same skills but feels completely new to students\n\n# LESSON INPUTS\nCountry = Australia\nClass Year = Grade P\nContext = \n[Skills] = [ Identifying the next shape in a growing pattern.\nRecognising repeating patterns with shapes ]\n\n# INSTRUCTIONS\n**HIGHEST PRIORITY: QUESTION STEM TRANSFORMATION**\n- MANDATORY: Every \"question\" field MUST use a completely different interrogative frame from the original. This is NON-NEGOTIABLE.\n- If original uses \"What is...?\", you MUST use something like \"Determine...\", \"Calculate...\", \"Which value represents...?\", \"By how much does... exceed...?\", etc.\n- If original uses \"How many...?\", you MUST use something like \"Calculate the number of...\", \"What quantity of...?\", \"Determine how much...\", etc.\n- FORBIDDEN: Reusing the exact same question starter/frame as the original, even with different nouns.\n- CRITICAL: All scenarios must remain within the  setting. Do NOT create generic scenarios that lose the context.\n\n**SECONDARY CONSTRAINTS:**\n- Generate content in the same format and style as the original content but with completely different examples\n- Do not copy the original content\n- Use alternative formulations that still produce a single numeric answer while testing the same skill, such as: imperative forms (\"Compute\", \"Convert\", \"Determine\", \"Express\"), inverse orientation (solve for the missing input instead of the output), comparative/difference prompts, or constraint-based directives. Maintain the same difficulty.\n- Do NOT copy any contiguous 3-word phrase from the original question text into the new question text, excluding numbers, units, and symbols.\n- Do not change the format of the original content\n- Keep the difficulty level (how hard it is to solve a problem) to Year Grade P level\n- Keep the reading level (how easy it is to read terms/words) to Year Grade P level\n- Keep the main content length/word count exactly same as original content\n- Keep the same number of emojis as present in original content\n- Keep the same real-world context/scenario as specified in the Context field above\n\t\n- IMPORTANT: Keep supplementary content fields (answers, explanations, tips, etc.) updated for new content\n- IMPORTANT: If teacherTips is present, ensure it is always output as an array of strings, even if there is only one tip\n- Follow the original requirements of the content when generating the new content\n- Add a <wordCountReasoning> element at the beginning of the output XML where you explain how you've maintained the exact word count while fundamentally changing the content\n- Add a \"regenerationReasoning\" field after \"wordCountReasoning\" that explains your strategy for creating fundamentally different content\n- If there is a \"question\" and \"answer\" field, add a \"answerReasoning\" field in between to work out the solution step-by-step before presenting the answer\n\n# REGENERATION STRATEGY\n1. **Identify the core mathematical skill** being tested in the original content\n2. **Analyze the original approach** - how does it present the problem?\n3. **Create a fundamentally different scenario** within the same context that tests the same skill\n4. **Use different numbers, examples, and problem structures** while keeping the same context and difficulty\n5. **Ensure students would see this as entirely new content**, not just minor variations\n6. **MANDATORY: Transform every question stem**: You MUST change the interrogative frame/starter. This is the PRIMARY goal.\n7. **Verify stem transformation**: Double-check that ZERO questions use the same starter as the original (e.g., if original was \"What is...\", new cannot be \"What is...\"). Also ensure no contiguous 3-word sequence (excluding numbers/units) from the original question appears in the new question.\n\n# REASONING FIELD GUIDANCE\nThe \"regenerationReasoning\" field should be a brief active thought process about transforming question stems:\n- \"I am maintaining the same  and [specific skills] while using different question structures.\"\n- \"I am creating new  scenarios with stems like '[new question 1]', '[new question 2]', etc.\"\n\n# ORIGINAL PROMPT INSTRUCTIONS (REFERENCE MATERIAL)\n- The following original prompt instructions are provided ONLY to help you understand the high-level style, requirements, and essence of the original content\n- These should inform your content creation approach but DO NOT override the specific formatting and length requirements specified above\n- Use this only as reference for content style and organization, NOT for output length or formatting\n<original_prompt_instructions>\n\n# REQUIREMENTS\n1. The activity must:\n   - Be completely self-contained, with no external prep required by the teacher beforehand.\n   - Be collaborative, encouraging teamwork and discussion.\n   - Be game-like, making math engaging.\n   - Align naturally with the given [Skills].\n   - Does NOT require teacher preparation, such as external datasets, maps, worksheets, or physical props.\n   - If any setup is needed (e.g., drawing number lines, creating grids, making maps), include these as initial steps for students to complete using the assumed materials.\n   - IMPORTANT: Never include steps where students \"receive\" materials from the teacher beyond the basic assumed materials listed below.\n   - IMPORTANT: You do NOT need to incorporate every skill in [Skills] into a single activity. When skills aren't closely related, select a subset of related skills that work well together for a cohesive activity.\n   - IMPORTANT: AVOID activities with 'treasure'. Think of something else.\n\n2. The activity output must include:\n   - picked_skills → List of the specific skills from [Skills] that this activity focuses on.\n   - header → Short, catchy title (max 30 characters).\n   - scenario → A short, engaging story that sets up the real-world application.\n   - steps → A list of clear, structured instructions (typically 3-5 steps, but adjust based on what the activity needs).\n   - discussion → Thought-provoking discussion questions to spark classroom dialogue (typically 2 questions).\n   - differentiation → Two options: one to support and one to extend learning.\n\n# ASSUMED MATERIALS\nThe following basic materials can be used in activities (no additional prep required):\n- Writing tools (pens, pencils)\n- Paper (standard A4/Letter size)\n- Basic geometry tools (ruler, protractor, compass)\n- Scissors\n- Calculator (appropriate for grade level)\n- Whiteboard/chalkboard (for teacher use)\n- Graph paper\n\n# INSTRUCTIONS\n1. Reason about how to create the best activity for the given [Skills].\n  - Choice behind the skills you pick and the ones you exclude\n  - Identifying the best real-world scenario for applying the skills\n  - How you will structure the activity to be self-contained and easy to follow.\n  - Any other considerations you need to take into account.\n  - AVOID activities with 'treasure'. Think of something else.\n2. Pick the best Skills from [Skills] that work well together for a cohesive activity.\n3. Create a short, catchy title for the activity in the 'header' field.\n4. Create a short, engaging story that sets up the real-world application.\n5. Structure the activity into a self-contained, easy-to-follow format, ensuring it fits one classroom slide.\n6. Ensure step-by-step clarity — students should be able to follow instructions independently.\n7. Consider any setup or materials needed - convert teacher prep work into student steps using assumed materials.\n8. Add a game element (scoring, time limits, extra challenges) to boost engagement.\n\n</original_prompt_instructions>\n\n# WORD COUNT ENFORCEMENT PROCESS\n- Before generating your response, you MUST follow this hidden verification process:\n  1. For each paragraph in the original content, count exact words\n  2. For each paragraph you write, match the EXACT word count\n  3. If word counts don't match, revise until they do\n  4. This verification must happen but should NOT appear in your final output\n\n# REVISION GUIDANCE\n- If a paragraph is too long: Remove details, examples, or explanations\n- If a paragraph is too short: Add minimal relevant context without changing meaning\n\n# KaTeX FORMATTING GUIDELINE\n- You will use the $ symbol for entering and exiting the KaTeX mode. For eg. $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n- Mathematical expressions and numbers within text should be wrapped in $ delimiters, but regular text should not be.\n- Remember to escape '%' when inside KaTeX mode, like '$\\%$', If you encounter '%' outside KaTeX mode wrap it with $\\%$ to confirm it to correct KaTeX.\n- Fraction Formatting: Always use '\\dfrac' instead of '\\frac' for all fractions to ensure they are displayed in more readable 'displaystyle' format.\n- ### CURRENCY FORMATTING\n  - **Dollar Values**: Always prefix monetary values with a dollar sign. The dollar sign must be escaped and the entire value wrapped in KaTeX.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$x$ has $\\$500$, and $y$ has $\\$300$ in bank'\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$\\$5', '$\\$10', '$\\$2.50'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$5', '5 dollars'\n  - **Cent Values**: For values less than a dollar, use whole numbers followed by \"c\" or \"cents\". Avoid using decimal notation like '$0.50'. This is critical for younger grade levels.\n    - ✅ **Correct:** '$50$c', '$50$ cents'\n    - ❌ **Incorrect:** '$0.50'\n- Whenever the context involves cost, price, or uses the word \"costing\", always prepend a dollar sign to the number and format it as $\\$<number>$ in KaTeX. For example: \"each costing $\\$7$\"\n  - **Context**: Apply these rules whenever the context involves money, cost, price, or value. For example: \"a coin worth $\\$2$\", \"an item costing $\\$7.50$\".\n- Make sure to prioritize writing text outside KaTeX mode, try to split up the section around the text. WRITE $x=4$ years $y=3$ NOT $x=4 \\text{years} y=3$.\n- Units should be written without delimiters and \\text function, please format according to the example below.\n  - eg input: \"$x$ is $5 \\text{m}$, and $y$ is only $2 \\text{cm}^2$ with angle $30^\\circ$\"\n  - eg output: \"$x$ is $5$ m, and $y$ is only $2$ cm$^2$ with angle $30^\\circ$\"\n- Use KaTeX from simple numbers to expressions to all things math that should be in KaTeX, except text, prioritize text outside the KaTeX mode.\n- For numbers $1000$ and above, use thin spaces instead of commas as thousand separators (e.g. $1\\,234$, $12\\,345$, $123\\,456$) following international standards.\n- Do not use any HTML tags (like <b>, <i>, <strong>, etc.) or markdown formatting (like **, *, _, etc.) in the text.\n- <EXTREMELY IMPORTANT> It is absolutely imperative that you MUST use KaTeX for mathematical expressions. Otherwise the Renderer will FAIL. </EXTREMELY IMPORTANT>\n\n# XML FORMAT\n- Output in XML format with <root> tag, including KaTeX with $ delimeters\n- IMPORTANT: All text content must be plain text only:\n-   NO tables or ASCII art\n-   NO markdown formatting\n-   NO line breaks (\\n)\n-   NO bullet points or lists\n-   NO special characters for formatting (|, -, *, >)\n-   NO indentation or spaces for formatting\n- Instead, write everything as continuous text using periods and commas for separation\n- DO NOT CHANGE THE STRUCTURE/FIELDS at any cost, all fields must remain the exact same as the original content\n\n# Original content:\nBanner Relay Race\nPicture the school organising a bright parade where each group carries signs in a hidden pattern. Work together so the procession stays orderly and lively through today!\n\nSteps\n1. Start drawing $3$ rows of flags, choosing a pattern that grows or loops (example: $1$ star, $2$ stars, $3$ stars quickly).\n2. Ask a partner to join you in extending the parade by adding rows, using the flag order you designed.\n3. Look at the new flag rows and decide if your teammate selected the right shapes to match your pattern.\n4. Award $1$ mark whenever your partner completes a flag row that copies the entire intended sequence correctly perfectly.\n5. Switch roles, and the other student designs a flag pattern for the next parade round!\n\nDiscussion\n• Describe how you can tell which flag comes next when you look at the parade rows in order.\n• Determine if the flag pattern repeats exactly or changes just a little whenever another row is added during today’s school parade.\n\nDifferentiation Opportunities\n• Support: Support: Start with flag sequences that include only $2$ shapes (such as star and triangle) to simplify ordering for everyone.\n• Extend: Extend: Build parade rows using $4$ or more flag shapes, and add challenges like rotating colours for each additional row during the event.\n\n\n\n# Output schema\n<root>\n\t<header></header>\n\t<scenario></scenario>\n\t<!--steps is a list, output repeated tags for each item as required -->\n\t<steps></steps>\n\t<steps></steps>\n\t<!--discussion is a list, output repeated tags for each item as required -->\n\t<discussion></discussion>\n\t<discussion></discussion>\n\t<differentiation>\n\t\t<support></support>\n\t\t<extend></extend>\n\t</differentiation>\n</root>\n","nodeType":"*lessonplan.ActivityNode","Country":"Australia","Strand":"Algebra","Duration":"0.00 minutes","YearOrder":"0","ContentDescriptor":"","PreviousLessonSkills":"","maxCompletionTokens":"16384","Regenerate":"true","isReasoningModel":"true"}
